{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.750263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00983a69185303a40dc30bdc0b9f1ccb02c0ed02122d33d1ca20d3582db17031","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:fokker_superpo_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ and let $v:(0,\infty)\to L^{1}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ be a non-negative function

such that 
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto b\big(t,x,v_t( x)\big) v_t(x)\big], \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto \sigma \sigma^*\big(t,x,v_t( x)\big) v_t(x) \big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty)\times\mathbb{R}^d).
\end{equation}

Then:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] If $X$ is a solution to MKV$(b,\sigma;\nu)$, with $ [X_t] = v_t (x) d x$ for any $t\geq0$, then $v$ is a weak solution to D-NL-FP$(b,\sigma;\nu)$.
\item[(ii)] [Superposition] Under the additional assumption that the functions 
\begin{equation}
\Big[ t \mapsto \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} \Big( \big|b\big(t,x,v_t(x)\big)\big| +\big\| \sigma \sigma^\top\big(t,x,v_t(x)\big) \big\| \Big)v_t(x) \, d x\Big], \ \Big[  t \mapsto  \int_{\mathbb{R}^d}v_t(x) \, d x\Big] \  \in L^{\infty}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty),
\end{equation}

if $v$ is a (weak) solution to D-NL-FP$(b,\sigma;\nu)$, 
then there exists a solution $X$ to D-MKV$(b,\sigma;\nu)$ such that $ [X_t] =v_t(x) d x $.

\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.665686+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_00d8cd3a8f00ce726592db90b2d933a2367be1a1b4e22b429d10bf3e2eafcba6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 3 (Almost-flat topological theorem) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] If a metric \(g\) on a closed three-manifold satisfies
\begin{equation}
        \bigl(\sup_M|\sec_g|\bigr)\diam(M,g)^2<\eps_{\mathrm{af}},
\end{equation}
then the manifold is an infranilmanifold; in particular, its fundamental group
is virtually nilpotent. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224134+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01238ee5ac0f7429ba463e8e37f96c84fd12b5d4a2cf500ce2bc44a3f9d2403a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary Cor:conformal of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $(M^n , [\hat{h}])$ be the conformal infinity of an asymptotically hyperbolic manifold $(X^{n+1},g^+ )$ such that $\Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) < \Lambda_{\gamma} (\mathbb{S}^n ,[h_{0}])$. Then, there exist constants $C>0$ and $\theta \geq 0$, depending on $M$ and $[\hat{h}]$, such that
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{I}_\gamma (h) - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \geq C \left( \frac{\inf\{\|h - \tilde{h}\|: \tilde{h} \in \mathcal{M}  \} }{\operatorname{vol}_{h}(M)^{1/2^*} }   \right)^{2+\theta} \quad \forall h \in [\hat{h}].
\end{align}
Moreover, when $\Lambda_\gamma (M,[\hat{h}]) > 0$ and the deficit $\mathcal{I}_\gamma (h) - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \leq 1$, there exist constants $C>0$ and $\theta \geq 0$, depending on $M$ and $[\hat{h}]$, such that
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{I}_\gamma (h) - \Lambda_{\gamma} (M,[\hat{h}]) \geq C \left( \frac{\inf\{\|h - \tilde{h}\|_*: \tilde{h} \in \mathcal{M}  \} }{\operatorname{vol}_{h}(M)^{1/2^*} }   \right)^{2+\theta} \quad \forall h \in [\hat{h}].
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:52:32.332989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_012f1cd96acc7b6fec5fbdd62471f0da28cf427a90d60c644d54863eaa5960c4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop: conv-zeta-signed of arXiv:2609.03959, 'The combinatorial Hopf algebra of signed graphs'] The balanced Whitney number polynomial may be obtained by the convolution of the deformed character $\zeta_x$:
\begin{equation}
 w_\Sigma^b(x,2t) = \zeta^{\ast t}_x (\Sigma).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:19:56.668871+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0166e1d290e5d09382b982771d6943739975ccd352c879696e8cf2d67e28311e","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2608.30875, 'A Decomposition Theorem for Topological Semi-small Maps'] A continuous proper surjection $ f: X \longrightarrow Y$ between path-connected, locally compact, Hausdorff spaces $X$ and $Y$ is called an \textbf{almost covering map}
     if 
     \begin{enumerate}
     	\item there exists a nowhere closed dense set $R \subset Y$ such that the restriction $f \vert_{f^{-1}(Y \setminus R)} : f^{-1}(Y \setminus R) \longrightarrow
     	Y \setminus R$ is a finite covering map, and
     	\item there is a finite decomposition $R=\bigsqcup_{\beta}T_{\beta}$ such that the restriction $f \vert_{f^{-1}(T_{\beta})}:f^{-1}(T_{\beta}) \longrightarrow T_{\beta}$ is a fiber bundle for each $\beta$.
     \end{enumerate}
     The set $Y \setminus R$ is called the regular set of the almost covering map $f$, and the set $R$ is referred to as a fibered set of the map $f$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.514074+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0195ca7628527b56c51de827afe7356b4bc93a14176716cbad76c7764c8c5029","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop poc of arXiv:2411.11759, 'Milstein-type schemes for McKean--Vlasov SDEs driven by Brownian motion and Poisson random'] If Assumptions \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:ic of the paper)} to \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:con of the paper)}    are satisfied, then the rate of convergence of the interacting particle system (equation eq:int of the paper) to the non-interacting particle system (equation eq:non-int of the paper)  is given~by
		\begin{align*}
			\sup_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}}\,\,\sup_{ t\in[0,T]}E|x_t^i-x_t^{i, N}|^2\leq K
			\begin{cases}
				N^{-1/2}, & \mbox{ if }  d <4,
				\\
				N^{-1/2}\ \log_2 N, & \mbox{ if } d=4,
				\\
				N^{-2/d}  &  \mbox{ if }d>4,
			\end{cases}
		\end{align*}
		where $K>0$ is a constant independent of $N$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.447807+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01b58fecbb056afc02715376f6d8bb07443e5effacf1425e6d633d0549d9b4ad","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 of arXiv:2606.15843, 'Long-time behaviour of DLRA for SDEs'] Suppose Assumptions (H3) and (H7) hold. 
	For any initial condition $\zeta\in L^2(\Omega,V_r(\bb R^d))$ and $\xi\in L^2(\Omega,\bb R^r)$ and any $T\ge0$, there exists a constant $C(b,\sigma,T)$ such that 
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            &\bb E_0[\|U^{(\mu)}_t\|^2_\rrm{HS}]=\zeta;\\
            &\bb E_0[|Y^{(\mu)}_t|^2]\le C(b,\sigma,T)\big(|\xi|^2+1\big),
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    for all $t\in[0,T]$. In particular,
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            &\bb E[\|U^{(\mu)}_t\|^2_\rrm{HS}]=r;\\
            &\bb E[|Y^{(\mu)}_t|^2]\le C(b,\sigma,T)\big(\bb E[|\xi|^2]+1\big),
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
    holds for every $t\in[0,T]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.528456+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_01f74deecadc171a72f7c13650a69bb6c54ff8e99171e3a2945af723c284d28b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.18557, 'Randomly Permuted Orthogonal Products and Fast Dimension Reduction'] Let \(P\) be a rank-\(m\) orthogonal projection on \(\R^d\), let $\rho=\max_{j\in[d]} P_{jj}$, and let \(q\) be a probability measure on \([d]\). Set
	\[ \sigma^2 = \frac{\rho \|q\|_2^2}{d}, \qquad M=\rho\|q\|_\infty.\]
	If \(\pi\) is uniform on \(S_d\), then
	\[
	\probb{\pi}{\norm{PD_{q_\pi}P - \frac{1}{d}P}\geq t}
	\le
	2m\exp\left(
	-\frac{t^2}
	{12\sigma^2+4\sqrt2\,Mt}
	\right)
	\qquad \text{for }t\geq0.
	\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.114825+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02031d21350de17cabf1c39b051ff4dcd4391f04d6496718dcdf655db8650efb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary COR35 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Under the same assumptions as in Theorem~(THM3.2 of the paper), the following estimates hold true: for any $\delta,\rho>0$, 
\begin{equation}
    {\mathbb{P}}^x[\sigma_B\le t]\ge \mathrm{Cap}_{(0)}(B_{x,\delta/2})\cdot \widetilde C_3\cdot \exp\left\{ -C_2 (1+\rho) \left( \frac{\widetilde{d}(x,B)+\delta}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta -1)}\right\}, \quad \forall 0<t\le T,
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde C_3>0$ is a constant depending on $T,\alpha, \beta, \delta$ and $\rho$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.661958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_020f76231cc4d3da1b8aca58aa595886d8ae27a7c551e348333a5646dc89d13d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Given $1<p<\infty$ and a matrix weight $W$, we say that $W$ satisfies the $A_p$ condition (denoted by $W\in A_p$) if
		\begin{equation}
			[W]_{A_p}:=\sup_{n\geq 1}\|\widetilde W_n\widehat W_n\|^p<\infty.
		\end{equation}
Similarly, we say that $W$ satisfies the $A_1$ condition (denoted by $W\in A_1$) if
\begin{equation}
[W]_{A_1}:=\sup_{n\geq 1}\|\widetilde{W}_nW^{-1}\|<\infty.
\end{equation}
The number $[W]_{A_p}$ is called the $A_p$ characteristic of $W$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.288597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_022d70d9baf46cd0e7a99a0c27f36f7d3cf4fa277b0deeef628f0eea4eb47fe1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:results:lyap of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Fix $d, \kappa \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}}$ with $\kappa \geq 2$ and $\lambda \in \mathcal{Q}_{\kappa, d}$, where
$\mathcal{Q}_{\kappa, d}$ is 
the set of admissible measures
from Definition  (def:results:entropyVariance of the paper). 
Let $(U, W)$ satisfy Assumption (as:results:lyapunov of the paper) and let $\mathbb H_\kappa$ and $\mathbb I_\kappa$ be as defined in (equation eq:results:entropy of the paper) and (equation eq:results:info of the paper), respectively. If $(\mu, \gamma)$ is a linear growth solution to the $\kappa$-MLFE{} on $[0, T]$ with potentials $(U, W)$ and initial condition $\lambda$ in the sense of Definition (def:not:linGrowth of the paper), then the following energy dissipation identity, 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{H}_\kappa(\mu_t) - \mathbb{H}_\kappa(\mu_r) =  - \int_r^t \mathbb{I}_\kappa(\mu_s) ds, 

    \end{equation}
holds for almost all $0 < r < t < T$. 
     In particular, $\mathbb{H}_\kappa$ is decreasing along the measure flow $t \mapsto \mu_t$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:58:01.556558+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02a58dce321dafd201b0d839a0d55a278c5febdfe97b18c8d79a6db1b571d6a9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 8 of arXiv:2608.27156, 'Traveling Waves in Equity Markets with\\Rank-Based Entry and Exit'] Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:wave}, the profile satisfies $1 - V(y) = e^{-\alpha y (1 + o(1))}$ as $y \to \infty$, where
\begin{equation}
    \alpha \;=\; \frac{a + \sqrt{a^{2} - 2\,\sigma^{2}(1)\,\tilde f'(1)}}{\sigma^{2}(1)},
    \qquad a \;=\; c - \tilde b(1):
\end{equation}
the capitalization distribution of the wave has a Pareto tail with exponent $\alpha$ at the top of the market. Symmetrically, $V(y) = e^{\beta y (1 + o(1))}$ as $y \to -\infty$, where $\beta$ is the positive root of \eqref{eq:char-poly} at $e = 0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.299601+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02c4b49a0baff5efdf368ea314b51d021e8b022e8717c3479d32c3f80abaca20","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2606.23383, 'Near-Lipschitz stability of the Kim--Milman flow map'] For any probability measure $\mu$ with $\mom(\mu) \defeq (\int \|x\|^2\,\mu(\!\dd x))^{1/2} < \infty$ and any $t > 0$,
    \begin{align*}
        \FI(q_t^\mu\mmid \gamma)
        &\le e^{-2t}\,\mom^2(\mu) + \frac{e^{-4t}}{1-e^{-2t}}\,d\,.
    \end{align*}
    In particular, if $\mom(\mu) \le \sqrt d$,
    \begin{align*}
        \FI(q_t^\mu\mmid \gamma)
        &\le \frac{de^{-2t}}{1-e^{-2t}}\,.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.645058+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02de625a63fd2530b632a8378c4eb4ff3c178398f2875b8e0fec04cd696b22f7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main of arXiv:2411.13926, 'Random walks on random walks:\\ non-perturbative results in high dimensions'] Consider the RWRW model  on $\mathbb{Z}^d$ with $\lambda \in [0,\infty)$ and  $d\geq 5$. 

\begin{itemize}
\item[a)] The Strong law of large numbers (SLLN):

There exists a  $v \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d$ such that
\begin{equation} \lim_{t \rightarrow \infty} \frac{X_t}{t} = v \quad P_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}}\text{-almost surely and in }L_1
\end{equation}

\item[b)]  Large deviation bounds (LDBs):

For every $\epsilon >0$ there exists constants $C,c>0$ such that 
\begin{equation}
P_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}} \left( \left| \frac{X_t}{t} - v \right| > \epsilon \right) \leq Ce^{-ct}, \quad \text{ for all } t \in \ensuremath{\mathbb{N}},
\end{equation}
with $v\in \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d$ as in (equation eq:SLLN of the paper).
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.301493+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02ec94df5e25c3cca62fdc048f3ba369abe189c4c179df46f581fbf42a3a1be4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:resultknownmask of arXiv:2602.11129, 'Information-Theoretic Thresholds for Bipartite Latent-Space Graphs Under Noisy Observation'] Consider any fixed $p \in (0,1)$. 
    Then, the following holds with probability $1 - o(n)$ over $\ \mathbf{M} \sim \mathbb{M}(n, m, q)$ whenever $d \gg \log(n)^3$.

    \vspace{0.5em}
    If $p \neq \frac{1}{2}$ then:\hspace{1em} $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \hspace{0.8cm}\dtv{\mathbb{W}_{\mathbf{M}}(n, m, p, d)}{ \mathbb{M}(n, m, p)} &= \begin{cases} 1 - o(1) & \text{if } d \ll nmq^2  \hspace{1.3cm} \text{ or } \hspace{.1cm} d \ll m\sqrt{n}q  \\
            o(1) & \text{if } d \gg nmq^2\log(n) \hspace{.1cm} \text{ and } \hspace{.1cm} d \gg m\sqrt{n}q \log(n)
            \end{cases}
        \end{aligned}$

    \vspace{0.5em}
    If $p = \frac{1}{2}$ then:\hspace{1em} $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \hspace{0.8cm}\dtv{\mathbb{W}_{\mathbf{M}}(n, m, p, d)}{ \mathbb{M}(n, m, p)} &= \begin{cases} 1 - o(1) & \text{if } d \ll nmq^2 \\
            o(1) & \text{if } d \gg nmq^2\log(n)
            \end{cases}
        \end{aligned}$

    \vspace{0.5em}
    \noindent Here, $o(1)$ denotes a function that tends to zero as $n \rightarrow \infty$. Recall further that $m \ge n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.734875+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02f0dc2aa6bfd7e4182c82fb7aeb04d3db182eae254d2f0a7a4bf56490fd327b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 21 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] We define the linear map $\tilde\Delta^\partial : \scal{\tcT} \to \scal{\tcT} \otimes \scal{\tcT_1}$ recursively via
	\begin{align*}
		&\tilde \Delta^\partial \<bs(2)0> = \<s(1)0> \otimes \partial \, \<b1> + \<b1> \otimes \partial \, \<s(1)0>, \qquad && \tilde \Delta^\partial \<ps(2)0> = \<s(1)0> \otimes \partial \, \<p1> + \<p1> \otimes \partial \, \<s(1)0>,
		\\
		&\tilde \Delta^\partial \<b0> = 0, \qquad &&\tilde \Delta^\partial \<p0> = 0,
		\\
		& \tilde \Delta^\partial \<s(1)0> = \mathbf{1} \otimes \partial \, \<s(1)0>, \qquad && \tilde \Delta^\partial X^k = 0,
		\\
		&\tilde \Delta^\partial \tau \sigma = \tilde \Delta^\partial \tau \tilde \Delta \sigma + \tilde \Delta \tau \tilde \Delta^\partial \sigma,
		\\
		&\tilde \Delta^\partial \mcI_\mfl^k \tau = (\mcI_\mfl^k \otimes \id) \tilde \Delta^\partial \tau + \sum_{|j|_\mfs < o(\mcI_\mfl^k \tau) } \frac{X^j}{j!} \otimes \mcI_{(\mfl, \partial)}^{k+j} \tau.
	\end{align*}
	For each $x,y \in \mbR^d$, we also define a linear map ${\chi}_x^y : \langle \tcT_1 \rangle \to \mbR$ as follows. For each $\mfl \in \mfL_+, \mft \in \mfL_-$ we set
	\begin{align*}
		\chi_z^{z'} (\mcI_{(\mfl, \partial)}^k \sigma) &= k! \mcQ_{X^k} [ J^\mfl(z') H_{\sigma; \eps, \delta}^{z, g}(z') + \mathcal{N}_{\gamma_{\sigma}}^{\mfl} H_{\sigma; \eps, \delta}^{z, g}(z')] 
		\\
		& \qquad - \sum_{|l|_\mfs < |\mcI_{\mfl}^k \sigma|_\mfs} \frac{(z'-z)^l}{l!} D^{k+l} K_\mfl \ast \mcR H_{\sigma; \eps, \delta}^{z, g}(z).
	\end{align*}
	We also define
	\begin{align*}
		\chi_z^{z'}(\Xi_{(\mft, \partial)}) &= \begin{cases}
			g_\eps(z') \qquad & \text{ if } \mft = \<b0>,
			\\
			g_{\delta}(z') \qquad & \text{ if } \mft = \<p0>,
			\\
			\eps^{-1/2} \partial_x K \ast g_{\eps, \delta}(z') \qquad & \text{ if } \mft = \<s(1)0>.
		\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	Finally, we extend this definition by declaring that for $\eta \in \mcT_+$ we have
	\begin{align*}
		\chi_z^{z'}(\mcI_{(\mfl, \partial)}^k \sigma \cdot \eta) = \chi_z^{z'}(\mcI_{(\mfl, \partial)}^k \sigma) \cdot \gamma_{z'z}(\eta)
	\end{align*}
	where $\gamma_{z'z}$ is the element of $\mathcal{G}_+$ corresponding to $\Gamma_{z'z}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:02.037143+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_02f2c34bd4347fcd4ecab5f08ffc16959d24706c53ad3313bf5e3848ea0fae0c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2601.00119, 'The largest common subtree of two random trees'] Let $\mu$ be a measure on $\mathbb{N}$. A random plane tree $\tau$ is called a \emph{Bienaym\'{e} tree with offspring distribution $\mu$} if it satisfies the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(a)] The law of $\mathrm{k}_\varnothing(\tau)$ is $\mu$.
    \item[(b)] For all $k \geq 1$ such that $\mu(k) > 0$, conditionally given that $\mathrm{k}_\varnothing(\tau) = k$, the subtrees $\theta_1\tau,...,\theta_k\tau$ are i.i.d.~and with the same law as $\tau$.
\end{enumerate}
We frequently call (b) \emph{the branching property} of Bienaymé trees. If $\sum_{k \geq 0} k\mu(k) = 1$ and $\mu(0) > 0$, then we call $\tau$ and $\mu$ \emph{critical}, and it is well-known that $\tau$ is almost surely finite.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:47:07.704467+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03116396a48885f30caf86423f498c82a16847e3bb94f355fb7e270d9cf17b9d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2608.06038, 'Efron type identities\\ for stopping sets and Poisson hulls'] Suppose that $Z$ is a Markov set and let
  $f\colon \BX\to\R_+$ be measurable with
  \begin{displaymath}
    \lambda(1-e^{-f})<\infty.
  \end{displaymath}
  Then
  \begin{align}
    \BE \big[\exp[-\eta_Z(f)+\lambda_Z(1-e^{-f})]\big]=1.
  \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:57:51.764187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_031274e01cd7b5a3f3b52ff507d2fd818c17847fe53a020a068389e18f9b154e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Continuity of the half-line Robin form) of arXiv:2608.30211, '2608.30211'] Let $V\in L^\infty(\R_+)$ be real.  For $\beta\in[0,\infty)$ set
\begin{equation}
 \nu_\beta(V):=
 \inf_{h\in H^1(\R_+)\setminus\{0\}}
 \frac{\displaystyle
  \int_0^\infty\bigl(|h'|^2+Vh^2\bigr)\,dy
  +\beta |h(0)|^2}
 {\displaystyle\int_0^\infty h^2\,dy},
\end{equation}
and let $\nu_\infty(V)$ be the same infimum over
$H_0^1(\R_+)\setminus\{0\}$, without the boundary term.  Then
$\beta\mapsto\nu_\beta(V)$ extends continuously to the compactified
interval $[0,\infty]$.  More quantitatively, there is $C_V>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
 0\leq \nu_\infty(V)-\nu_\beta(V)\leq C_V\beta^{-1/2}
 \qquad(\beta\geq1).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:35:04.214534+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0332cc10fdbdc18fa14d2f3fd334b0a601b145d2f18cd8a75657699c7e404587","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 18 of arXiv:2608.03956, 'From Triangular Array Progression\\ to Near-Log-Concave State Transitions'] Let $\mathbf{D}$ be an ordered finite subset of $\R$ with cardinality $|\mathbf{D}|\geq 3$, and $X : \Omega \rightarrow \mathbf{D}$ be a random variable with probability mass function 
\begin{align}
\P\left(X = d_j\right) = P_{X}(d_j), \notag 
\end{align}
for every $d_j \in \mathbf{D}$. The random variable $X$ is called \emph{near}-log-concave of degree $r\in \Z_+$ for finite $0 \leq r \leq |\mathbf{D}|-3$, if 
\begin{align} 
P_{X}(d_{j-1})P_{X}(d_{j+1}) \leq P_{X}(d_j)^2 \hspace{0.15in} \text{$\forall j\in\mathcal{I}_{|\mathbf{D}|}^{(r)}$}, \notag
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{I}_{|\mathbf{D}|}^{(r)} \subseteq \mathcal{I}_{|\mathbf{D}|} = \{2,\ldots,|\mathbf{D}|\}$ such that 
\begin{align}
|\mathcal{I}_{|\mathbf{D}|}^{(r)}| = |\mathbf{D}| - r - 2. \notag
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.857056+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_035373c7e9fd88d3385217c684e0b0bc3edce44f63aa1046511bed0e09ab8d2e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mathcal Q$ be a semi-ring. Then
