{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.917045+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03c34e5753a66b1b11b687a7aae115c74d9bba1700a95f68351d55bc4b481d86","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 21 (Integral one-sided color expansion) of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] For every fixed $n\ge2$ and every zero-framed knot $K$, there are Laurent
polynomials
\begin{equation}
 b_k^{(n)}(K;q)=\varepsilon_n a_{(k)}(K;q^2)
 \in\Z[q^{\pm1}]
\end{equation}
such that, for every $r\ge0$,
\begin{equation}
 J_r^{SU(n)}(K;q)=
 \sum_{k=0}^{r}b_k^{(n)}(K;q)
 \prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(1-q^{2(r-j)}).
\end{equation}
Here $a_{(k)}(K;q^2)$ is the coefficient defined by the finite dual
formula in \textup{(BG3)}.  Explicitly, for each partition $\mu$ one has
the finite representationwise identity
\begin{equation}
 J_K(V(\mu);q^2)\id_{V(\mu)}
 =\left(\sum_{\lambda\subseteq\mu}
 a_\lambda(K;q^2)\sigma_\lambda\right)\Big|_{V(\mu)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.428044+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_03d5ea64fffbafeca54a37467def92d0dc61aa834de9f81f2952029b2312f9f6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:burning of arXiv:2605.19419, 'Tail exponents of the three-dimensional uniform spanning tree and Abelian sandpile'] Let $K\Subset\mathbb{Z}^{d}$ contain $0$ and all nearest neighbours of $0$. Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an event of subsets of $K$ such that every $A\in\mathcal{A}$ contains at least one nearest neighbour of $0$. Then
