Then, BSDE (equation BSDE1.1 of the paper) admits a unique $L^p$ solution $(y_t,z_t)_{t\in[0,\tau]}$ in the space of $H_\tau^p(0;{\mathbb R}^{k}\times{\mathbb R}^{k\times d})$. Moreover, if $|\xi|+\int_0^\tau |g(s,0,0)|{\rm d}s\in L^\infty(\Omega,\mathcal{F}_\tau, \mathbb{P};{\mathbb R})$, then $(y_t)_{t\in[0,\tau]}\in S^\infty({\mathbb R}^k)$.\vspace{0.1cm}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615760+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07afdcba9bb4f2101c6773a06a157b47da8d972cc5f693bcf192076d865bf77d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 (Isomorphism theorem) of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] For each $n\ge0$ and each~$t>0$ there exists a coupling of~$L_t$ and two copies~$h$ and~$\tilde h$ of~GFF on~$\T_n$ such that
\begin{equation}

h\independent L_t 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

L_t(x)+h(x)^2 = \bigl(\tilde h(x)+\sqrt t\bigr )^2
\end{equation}
holds pointwise a.s.\ for all~$x\in\T_n$. (Note: $\tilde h$ and~$L_t$ are not independent.) (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.749203+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07d8cf7b6a5f3f26026badc89470b5804422f12c0b0b2ae6bd9b3d52e129ce34","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:non_lin_MP of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. A probability measure $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$, on $(C_{\infty},\mathcal{B})$, is a solution to NL-MP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$ if it is a solution to MP$({\bf b}_{\boldsymbol\mu}, {\boldsymbol\sigma}_{\boldsymbol\mu};0,\nu)$ (see Definition (def:MP_lin of the paper)), with 
