{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.352435+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09d2a4ae5516d4dea2355a0331f3a9d63efe357488a439bc10a5fd5f3b25cb6a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] For each $\alpha>0$ and $d\in \mathbb{N}$, the \textbf{$d$-level Poisson-avoiding metric} with boundary parameter $\alpha$ is a random function
$$
H_d: \{(u, s; v, t) \in (\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R})^2 : s < t\} \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}
$$
defined as follows. First, let $\Pi$ be a Poisson process on $\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R}$ such that $\Pi|_{(x, a) \times \mathbb{R}}$ has intensity $\alpha$ if $x = 0$, and intensity $1$ if $x > 0$. From $\Pi$, we construct a random weighted directed graph where:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
    \item $(u, t)$ is connected to $(v, t)$ by an edge of weight $1$ if $(u, v)$ is a directed edge in $\Lambda_d$.
    \item $(u, s)$ is connected to $(u, t)$ by an edge of weight $0$ if $(u, r) \notin \Pi$ for all $r \in (s, t]$.
\end{itemize}
Let $H_d:(\Lambda_d \times \mathbb{R})^2 \to \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0},$ be directed graph distance in this metric.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.535200+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09e81a85064621168e4bf555ae9395be30f07eb491f45d3a9db14a57a203bf76","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2603.28170, 'Stabilization time of finite configurations with a second class particle in discrete TASEP'] For $p\in(0,1)\setminus\{1/2\}$ fixed, we have that
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_n^{(2)}-\max(p,1-p)n}{\sqrt n}\stackrel\d\Longrightarrow\mathcal N(0,p(1-p))
\end{equation}
in distribution as $n\to\infty$
where the limit is Gaussian with mean $0$ and variance $p(1-p)$.

For $p=1/2$,
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_n^{(2)}-n/2}{\sqrt n}\stackrel\d\Longrightarrow\frac{\chi_3}2
\end{equation}
in distribution as $n\to\infty$ where $\chi_3$ is the chi distribution with parameter $3$.

For $p=1/2+\lambda/(2\sqrt n)$ with $\lambda\in\R$ fixed, we have
\begin{equation}
\frac{T_n^{(2)}-n/2}{\sqrt n}\stackrel\d\Longrightarrow M_1^\lambda-\frac12B_1^\lambda
\end{equation}
in distribution as $n\to\infty$ where $B_t^\lambda$ is a Brownian motion with drift $\lambda$
and $M_t^\lambda=\max_{s\in[0,t]}B_s^\lambda$ is its running maximum process. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.343846+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_09ea7c676615d5c55da3f9c09d4805a53c721d6478c7d6d88a9384d4ff471fdf","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:disi of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume hypotheses and notation of Theorem~(thm:main of the paper).
For any $x\in H$ and $0<s\leq t$
it follows that
\begin{equation}
\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,P^*_t\mu_\ast)\right)^2\leq \int_{H}
\int_{H}\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_s\delta_z,P^*_s\delta_y)\right)^2 P^*_{t-s}\delta_x(\mathrm{d} z)\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} y).
\end{equation}
In particular, it follows that
\begin{equation}
\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,\mu_\ast)\right)^2=\left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,P^*_t\mu_\ast)\right)^2
\leq \int_{H} \left(\mathcal{W}_2(P^*_t\delta_x,P^*_t\delta_y)\right)^2\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} y).
