{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.711920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_179297a6b33b8385f9b8c8bf8e07ccd32bdbb69877e2941905867d45226dfc63","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2608.29736, '2608.29736'] Let $(M^n,\omega)$ be a compact K\"ahler manifold of complex
 dimension $n\geq 2$. Let $\chi$ be another K\"ahler form.  Suppose that the $J$-semistable condition ~\eqref{topintegralnonnegative}--~\eqref{$J$-semistablecondition} holds. Set the positive constant \begin{equation}
     c= \frac{2n\displaystyle\int_M\Big(\chi^n-n\chi^{n-1}\wedge\omega\Big)+\int_M \omega^n}{8n^2\displaystyle\int_M\chi\wedge\omega^{n-1}},
     \end{equation}and let \begin{equation}
     S:=\{ D \,|\,D\; \text{is a prime divisor and } \int_DF_{n-1}(\chi)< c\int_D\omega^{n-1} \}.
 \end{equation}
Then any finite subset of  $S$ is an exceptional family in the sense of Boucksom~\cite{Boucksom}. In particular, the cardinality of  $S$ is bounded by  the Picard number of $M$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.094935+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_17c28f87e5cba4b542cd8f92bb2be722dd7222750170de1a47e94133fa934e8f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo theo18 of arXiv:2609.02795, 'Global smooth behavior in Kuznetsov and Westervelt type\\ viscous wave equations:\\ A unif'] Let $n\ge 1$ and $\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ be a bounded domain with smooth boundary, let $a>0$,
  and suppose that
  \begin{equation} \label{h}
	h\in \bigcup_{\iota>0} C^{1+\iota}_{loc} (\mathbb{R}^2)
	\quad \mbox{is such that} \quad
	h(0,0)>0,
  \end{equation}
  that
  \begin{equation} \label{f0}
	\left\{ \begin{array}{l}
	f \in \bigcup_{\iota>0} C^{1+\iota}_{loc}(\mathbb{R}^{2n+2})
	\qquad \mbox{with} \\[1mm]
	f(0,0,0,0)=0 \mbox{ and } \nabla f(0,0,0,0)=0
	\end{array} \right.
  \end{equation}
  and that with some $\lambda>1$, for each $M>0$ there exists $K_f(M)>0$ such that
  \begin{equation} \label{f1}
	|f(\xi,\sigma,X,Y)|
	\le K_f(M) \cdot (1+ |X| + |Y| )^\lambda
	\quad \mbox{for all $(\xi,\sigma,X,Y)\in \mathbb{R}^{2n+2}$ such that } |\xi|+|\sigma|\le M.
  \end{equation}
  Then given any
  \begin{equation} \label{18.001}
	p>n\cdot\max\{\lambda,2\}
	\quad \mbox{such that} \quad
	p\ge 2\cdot\max\{\lambda,2\}

  \end{equation}
  and an arbitrary $r\ge\max\{p,2\lambda\}$ fulfilling
  \begin{equation} \label{18.01}
	r>2\lambda
	\qquad \mbox{if } n=2
  \end{equation}
  and
  \begin{equation} \label{18.02}
	\frac{np\lambda}{n+p-2} \le r < \frac{np}{n-2}
	\qquad \mbox{if } n\ge 3,
  \end{equation}
  one can find $\eta=\eta(p,r,a,h,f)>0$, $\beta=\beta(p,r,a,h,f)>0$ and $\Gamma=\Gamma(p,r,a,h,f)>0$ with the property that whenever
