{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:56:17.352559+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2e89c7623677510160810685cc7d282dee5ac90db0f38bc4811c2599924762c4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mPAM of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] For each $\epsilon>0$, we take $d_\epsilon>0$, such that $d_\epsilon\epsilon^{1/2}|\log(\epsilon)|\to 0$ as $\epsilon\to 0$.
Then consider the following function defined on $\mathbb{H}^2_\uparrow$:
\begin{equation}   
\mathcal{M}^{\rho,\epsilon}(x, s; y, t):= -\epsilon^{1/2}H_{d_\epsilon}^-(\lfloor 2\epsilon^{-1}x \rfloor, 2\epsilon^{-3/2}s;  \lfloor 2\epsilon^{-1}y \rfloor, 2\epsilon^{-3/2}t) + \epsilon^{-1}(t-s),
\end{equation}
where either (1) fixed $\alpha\ge \frac{1}{2}$ and $\rho=-\infty$, or (2) $\alpha = \frac{1}{2}-\rho\epsilon^{1/2}/2$ for some $\rho\in\mathbb{R}$.
