{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.052168+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2fb54415ece96d4382d95b69f0608aaa91bacd6c20ae3dcae22c1c360979b2af","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:fourier of arXiv:2607.12564, 'Power-law and log-periodic degree tails for a family of probability generating function eq'] Let $\chi:=2\pi/\log\lambda$, $s_m:=\rho+1+i\chi m$, and
$\mathcal{M}_j(s):=\int_0^\infty w_j(x)x^{s-1}dx=\mathbb{E}[V_j^{\,s-1}]$, finite and analytic for
$\operatorname{Re}s>1$. Then the continuous period-$1$ function $\Psi_j$ has Fourier coefficients
\begin{equation}
  \widehat\Psi_j(m):=\int_0^1\Psi_j(u)e^{2\pi i m u}\,du=\frac{2p}{a\log\lambda}\,\mathcal{M}_j(s_m).
\end{equation}
In particular $\widehat\Psi_j(0)=\overline\Psi_j=\frac{2p}{a\log\lambda}\mathbb{E}[V_j^{\,\rho}]>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.114998+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2fbdd03562cb75421c783afc10663ff676f070c8b13bd50061ee909a52508644","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4.2 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Let $X$ be a Borel right process on $(E,d)$ satisfying (AC), (H1a) and (H1b).

 Further assume that $X$ admits the following short-time heat kernel lower bound at a fixed point $x\in E$: For some $T>0, \alpha>0,\beta>1$, there exist constants $C_1,C_2>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
p(t,x,y)\ge \frac{C_1}{t^{\alpha/\beta}}\exp\left\{-C_2\left(\frac{d(x,y)}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta -1)}\right\}, \quad \forall t\in (0, T], \, y\in E.
\end{equation}
Let $B\subset E$ be a nearly Borel set of finite $1$-order capacity.
Then for any $\lambda, \delta,\rho>0$, 
 \begin{equation}
        \mathbb{P}^x[\sigma_B\leq t]\geq \frac{\mathrm{Cap}_{(\lambda)}(B_{x,\delta})}{\mathrm{e}^{\lambda T}}\cdot  \frac{C_1C_3}{ t^{(\alpha-\beta)/\beta}}\exp\left\{-(1+\rho)C_2\left( \frac{\widetilde{d}(x,B)+\delta}{t^{1/\beta}}\right)^{\beta/(\beta-1)}\right\},\quad 0<t\leq T,
    \end{equation}
    where $B_{x,\delta}:=\{y\in B:d(y,x)\leq \widetilde{d}(x,B)+\delta\}$, $C_1$ and $C_2$ are as in (equation eq:421 of the paper), and $C_3=(\beta-1)\rho\cdot \min\{(1+\rho)^{\alpha-\beta-\alpha/\beta}, 1\}>0$ is a constant depending on $\alpha, \beta$ and $\rho$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:40:22.275569+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2fbf87cf4bd49863ee400276f2257d8107a96d514ca0affa4cb686e908bc2318","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop rodl1995threshold of arXiv:2609.02588, 'Ramsey-type results for Maker-Breaker games'] Let $r\geq 2$ and let $H$ be a graph with a cycle. Then there exist a constant $C$ such that for $m=Cn^{2-1/m_2(G)}$ and any $t\geq 0$ the random graph $G(n,m)$ contains a subgraph $F'$ that satisfies the following with probability tending to $1$ as $n$ tends to infinity:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Every subgraph $G\subseteq F'$ with $v(G)\leq t$ satisfies $m_2(G)\leq m_2(H)$
        \item $F'\rightarrow_r H$
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.862228+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2fc2b6c1980ffa6703e3fcfdcb7b0063dc52e4f3ab607b487035d8c87218d726","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 40 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mf A$ be a sequentially complete HTG and $\mu$ be an $\mf A$-valued premeasure. Then
  \begin{multline}
    \Sigma_{\mf A}(\mu) =  \sett{A}{A\in \q\mu \mbox{ and the family $\mu\circ \mb A$ is summable in $\mf A$}}{\mbox{for every countable partition $\mb A$ of $A$ into elements of $D_\mu$}}.
  \end{multline}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.854807+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3013089afebb8b21030f9b3fb81f8c6bfdad2bb8ab38fbfd583b528bde326efa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 1 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mathcal Q$ be a $\delta$-ring. Then the following statements hold:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item [(i)] Let $B\in\mathcal Q$ and $\mb A\colon I\to \mathcal Q$ be a countable family such that $\mb A(i)\subset B$ for all $i\in I$. Then $\bigcup_{i\in I} \mb A(i) \in \mathcal Q$.
  \item [(ii)] $\sigma(\mathcal Q) = \sigma_0(\mathcal{Q})$.
  \item [(iii)] If $A\in \sigma(\mathcal Q)$ and $A\subset B$ for some $B\in \mathcal Q$, then $A\in \mathcal Q$.
  \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:01:01.834737+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30177890ad9200f04a7f7e2769212dd72571cef7801f525f404b8bdcca532602","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:exp-log-differentials-app of arXiv:2609.00556, 'Higgs--Demailly System and Positivity of Higgs Bundles'] For a Hermitian endomorphism $u$ and an endomorphism-valued one-form $X$,
\begin{align}
 e^{-u}\mathcal D(\exp)_u(X)
 &=\int_0^1e^{-su}Xe^{su}\,ds,
 \\
 \mathcal D(\log)_G(B)
 &=\int_0^\infty(G+\rho\operatorname{Id}_E)^{-1}B(G+\rho\operatorname{Id}_E)^{-1}\,d\rho.

\end{align}
If $u=\operatorname{diag}(\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_r)$ at a point, then
\begin{equation}
 \bigl(e^{-u}\mathcal D(\exp)_u(X)\bigr)_{ab}
 =\chi(\ell_b-\ell_a)X_{ab},
 \qquad \chi(s)=\frac{e^s-1}{s},
\end{equation}
with $\chi(0)=1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.751962+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_302b1cae16b8f43f86f4a5e453dca5e0d81b7b01a6ced6249d0957f3317be615","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:MP_lin of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $s\in [0,T)$ and $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$. A probability measure $\mathbb{P}=\mathbb{P}_{s,\nu}$, on $(C_{[s,T)},\mathcal{B})$, is a solution to MP$(b,\sigma;s,\nu)$ if: 

\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $x_{s} \sim  \nu$ under $\mathbb{P}$;
\item[(ii)] for any $\varphi\in C_0^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, the process defined by 
\begin{equation}
M_t : =\varphi(x_{t}) - \varphi(x_{s}) - \int_{s}^t \Big(   \langle  b(r, x_r) , \nabla \varphi(x_{r})  \rangle + \frac{1}{2}\text{tr}\big(\sigma \sigma^\top (r , x_r)  \nabla^2 \varphi(x_r)  \big) \Big) dr , \qquad t\in [s, T),
\end{equation}
is a martingale w.r.t. the natural filtration of $(x_t)_{t\in [s,T)}$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.859299+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_305453b7b1830fbf51285d3650dd6e8cc77a5a6a49463504601e923bf97f7661","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm main of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Suppose that $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ is a generalised preferential attachment tree. Then,
    \begin{enumerate} 
        \item  If \[\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{F(j)^2} = \infty \quad \text{almost surely},\] then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ almost surely does not have a persistent hub. 
        \item  Suppose that the following two conditions are satisfied: 
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item There exists a sequence $(x_{j})_{j \in \mathbb{N}_0}$ with $\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{x_{j}^2} < \infty$ such that,
				\begin{equation}            
             		F(j) \geq x_{j} \quad \text{almost surely.}
             	\end{equation} 
            \item There exists a $\lambda > 0$ such that 
                \begin{equation} 
                \sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \prod_{j=0}^{i}\E{\frac{F(j)}{F(j) + \lambda}} < \infty.
                \end{equation}
        \end{enumerate}
        Then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ has a unique persistent hub almost surely. 
        \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.580235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30583a82e7a16df5af95b9ff428b642af740b551cd0b869e6111f53b2a8e523a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:IndS of arXiv:2411.10333, 'Subcritical annulus crossing in spatial random graphs'] We call the following setup the \emph{independent setting}: 
		Let the underlying point process have independent increments and is thus either given by a Poisson point process, cf.~[LastPenrose2017], or by a Bernoulli site-percolated lattice. Further, for a given collection of mutually different vertex pairs \(\{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2}\},\dots,\{\boldsymbol{x}_{k_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\}\), we have 
	\begin{equation*}\tag{Ind}
\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\Big(\bigcap_{j=1}^k \{\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1}\sim \boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}\} \, \Big| \, \mathscr{V} \Big) = \prod_{i=1}^k \mathbf{p}(\boldsymbol{x}_{i_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{i_2}), 
	\end{equation*} 
	for a suitable, symmetric function \(\mathbf{p}\). Here, \(\mathbb{P}_\lambda^{\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\boldsymbol{x}_{1_2},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}}\) denotes the law where the given points \(\boldsymbol{x}_{1_1},\dots,\boldsymbol{x}_{k_2}\) have been added to the graph in the Poisson point process case, or the law conditioned on the event that said vertices survived the site percolation, respectively.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.750052+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30599e3673db3eb5efe5656bd6481e3cb73581694dd12ddcd15b8318cbb51d61","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:equivalence_nonlinear_mp_weak_sol_den of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ be a probability measure on $(C_{\infty}, \mathcal{B} )$, such that
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb{P}_{\nu}(x_t \in d y) = v_t(y) dy, 
 \qquad t> 0 ,
\end{equation}
and such that
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto b\big(t,x,v_t( x)\big) v_t(x)\big], \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto \sigma \sigma^\top \big(t,x,v_t( x)\big) v_t(x)\big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty)\times\mathbb{R}^d).
\end{equation}

Then $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ is a solution to D-NL-MP$(b,\sigma;\nu)$ if and only if there exists a (weak) solution $X$ to D-MKV$(b,\sigma;\nu)$ such that $[X] =\mathbb{P}_{\nu} $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:32:20.761026+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30cfa1e75265e5c45869a476ef0deadcb55bae945ef1197e993cbac7d6c0a744","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 15 of arXiv:2609.00448, 'Equivariant formality and representation theory'] \textnormal{(\cite[Theorem 1.3]{F2}).} Let $G$ act on a finite CW-complex $X$. Then the following are equivalent. 
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $X$ is an equivariantly formal $G$-space.
	\item The forgetful map $f: K_G^*(X; \mathbb{Q})\to K^*(X; \mathbb{Q})$ is surjective. 
	\item The forgetful map $f$ induces an isomorphism $K_G^*(X; \mathbb{Q})\otimes_{R(G; \mathbb{Q})}\mathbb{Q}\to K^*(X; \mathbb{Q})$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.752851+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30d224eeed4835375026e9ce27d7d2e1638d5627a08a194bc5a48819d89dd52d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 of arXiv:2605.14206, 'The clumsy coupon collector's problem'] For any $r > 0$, the tail event $\{ T_{m, p} \geq r \}$ has the following
  upper bound:
  \begin{align*}
      \PP(T_{m, p} \geq r)
      &\leq 2 - 2 
      \binom{m\exp(1/r)}{m}^{-1}
      (1-p)^m\\
      &\phantom{{} \leq 2 - 2} \times
      \left[
      1 - \int_0^1 p \, m
      \left(1-x\right)^{m\exp(1/r)-m}
      \left(1-p\,x\right)^{m-1} dx
      \right]^{-1}.
  \end{align*}
  \begin{proof}
    Since $T_{m, p}$ is non-negative,
    we can invoke a common bound from \cite[p.~126]{kallenberg2021probability},
    namely $\PP(T_{m,p} \geq r) \leq 2\left(1 - M_{T_{m,p}}(-1/r)\right)$.
    Substitute Lemma \ref{thm:factored mgf}.
  \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.414969+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_30f58ef7a36950c818eea0f5be9d04c1f6e2c04da028e530ff7bfd6fb404d489","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma two Killing vector fields of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] Let $k$ be a solution to (equation eq:EL for k of the paper) for $C^2> 0$ on $[0,L]$. By (equation eq:EL for k and tau of the paper), set $\tau =C k^{2-2p}$. Let $\gamma:[0,L]\to \R^3$ be an associated curve with tangent $T$, normal $N$ and binormal $B$. 
  Then the vector fields 
  \begin{equation*}
    J_0 = T ((p-1)k^p - \lambda) + N (p(p-1)k^{p-2}k') + B(pk^{p-1}\tau)\qquad \text{and} \qquad I = k^{p-1}B 
  \end{equation*}
  are Killing vector fields along $\gamma$.
  Moreover, $|J_0|^2 = (p-1-\lambda)^2 + p^2C^2$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.587495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_310dccc45ad15af6b9fb10a2f70c0c8e77c978f1365573c946f086bf882fe2f7","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem finite_energy_approx of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] For $\mathsf{P}\in  \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ with  $\mathcal F_{\beta}({P})<\infty$  there exists a sequence $\mathsf{P}^n\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ with $\mathcal{W}^{int}(\mathsf{P}^n)\leq \mathcal{W}^{int}(\mathsf{P})+1/n$, $\mathcal E(\mathsf{P}^n)\leq \mathcal E(\mathsf{P})+1/n$ and 
    \begin{align}
        \int \sum_{x\in \xi\cap \Lambda_1}\left\lvertx^+-x\right\rvert^p\mathsf{P}^n(d\xi)<\infty.
    \end{align}
    Furthermore, $\mathsf{P}^n\xrightarrow{n\to \infty}\mathsf{P}$  w.r.t. $\mathcal T_{\mathcal L}$ (in particular weakly) and for $n\in \IN$ there exists $q_n>0$  such that the measure $\mathsf{P}^n$ is concentrated on the set \begin{align}
        \Gamma_{q_n}=\{\xi\in \Gamma: \varphi(x-y)\leq q_n,1/q_n\leq \left\lvertx-y\right\rvert \text{ for any two distinct }x,y\in \xi \}.
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.662037+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_314cbd0eae2b3c34467750bb478083b4ca95904031829b05d485beacd19658f6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Let $1<p<\infty$. Given  a matrix weight $W\in A_p$, we have
