{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.223983+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_36cd0a579729f84bb65c1bff153a7078644c08fc3d17a34f06b69d242de9a40d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theoremletter Thm:Neumayer of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] Let $(M,g)$ be a closed Riemannian manifold of dimension $n\geq 3$ that is not conformally equivalent to the round sphere. Then, there exist constants $C>0$ and $\theta\geq 0$, depending on $(M,g)$, such that
    \begin{align*}
        Q(u)-Y(M,[g]) \geq C d(u,\mathcal{M})^{2+\theta} \quad \forall u \in H^1 (M,\mathbb{R}_+ ),
    \end{align*}
    where $d(-,\mathcal{M})$ is a suitable distance to the set of minimizers $\mathcal{M}$. Moreover, there exists an open dense subset $\mathcal{G}$ in the $C^2$ topology on the space of smooth conformal classes of metrics on $M$ such that if $[g]\in \mathcal{G}$ then $\theta = 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

