{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.739958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_390acdf299480cc709a5319f3f242dbf60046a09224f038db7e1dbcd69d549e8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 47 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Suppose that $f \in \cB_{p,q}^{\alpha - \beta}$ where $\alpha \in \mbR$ and $p,q \in [1,\infty]$. Then given a $\beta$-regularising kernel $K$ in the sense of \cite[Assumption 5.1]{H0}, we have that \begin{align*}
