    \vspace{0.5em}
    If $p = \frac{1}{2}$ then:\hspace{1em} $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \dtv{\mathbb{W}(n, m, q, p, d)}{ \mathbb{M}(n, m, p)} &= \begin{cases} 1 - o(1) & \text{if } d \ll nmq^4 \\
            o(1) & \text{if } d \gg nmq^4\log(n)
            \end{cases}
        \end{aligned}$

    \vspace{0.5em}
    \noindent Here, $o(1)$ denotes a function that tends to zero as $n \rightarrow \infty$. Recall further that $m \ge n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:01:48.560896+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_433e7602b9a27d9abd0a4383a526722abedc14f92f0d9a1bee5c20935d4999e2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:boundedness_dot_product_B of arXiv:2606.29622, 'Affine Structure of the Brownian Signature'] {Fix $T > 0$ and $\bm{p} \in \mathcal B$. There exists a constant $C_T > 0$ such that for any continuous $\mathbb{R}$-valued semimartingale $X = (X_t)_{t \in [0, T]}$ and for all $0 \leq s \leq t \leq T$,}
