{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.284547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4a70916486fc5333e3846cdc32389b62c79b1f6876e2b0640d2e44bb1882faa1","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem l1bound of arXiv:2604.20629, 'Rates of forgetting for the sequentially Markov coalescent'] Let $\{q_{x}(\cdot):x>0\}$ be a family of probability
densities on $(0,\infty)$, and regard $x\mapsto q_{x}(\cdot)$ as
a map from $(0,\infty)$ into $L^{1}(0,\infty)$.
Assume there exist continuously differentiable functions $A,B:(0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$
and an integrable function $\Phi:(0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ such that
the following all hold:
\begin{gather}
q_{x}(t)=A(x) \Phi(t) \mathbf{1}_{\{0<t<x\}}+B(x) e^{-t} \mathbf{1}_{\{t\ge x\}},\qquad t>0.\\
\lim_{t\uparrow x}q_{x}(t)=A(x)\Phi(x)=B(x)e^{-x}=\lim_{t\downarrow x}q_{x}(t).\\
\partial_{x}q_{x}(t):=A'(x) \Phi(t) \mathbf{1}_{\{0<t<x\}}+B'(x) e^{-t} \mathbf{1}_{\{t>x\}}\in L^{1}(0,\infty)\\
\sup_{x>0}\|\partial_{x}q_{x}\|_{L^{1}}\le L<\infty.
\end{gather}
Then $x\mapsto q_{x}$ is absolutely continuous as an $L^{1}$-valued
function, and for all $a,b>0$, 
\begin{equation}
q_{b}-q_{a}=\int^{b}_{a}\partial_{x}q_{x} dx,\quad\text{in \ensuremath{L^{1}.}}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, for all $a,b>0$, 
\begin{equation}
\|q_{b}-q_{a}\|_{L^{1}}\le L |b-a|.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:15:34.386022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4a983c7f5f20520f0b0c916955e5171c73a8de0c039ba4cddc41de17cefa967f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 4 (\cite{keel1992}) of arXiv:2609.03268, 'Poincare Polynomials of Heavy-Light Hassett Spaces'] Let $R=\Z[x_I: 2\le |I| \le n-2]$ be the polynomial ring over $\Z$ on $2^n-2n-2$ variables. Then there is a quotient map $R\onto A(\DMumford{n})$ given by $x_I\mapsto [D_I]$. Furthermore, the kernel of this quotient map is the ideal generated by the following elements:
            \begin{enumerate}
                \item $x_I-x_{I^c}$ for all $I$;
                \item $x_I x_J$ for each pair $I,J$ such that $\varnothing\notin \{I\cap J, I \cap J^c, I^c\cap J, I^c \cap J^c\};$
                \item (WDVV relations) for $\{a,b,c,d\}\in {[n]\choose 4}$, 
                $$\sum_{I: I\cap\{a,b,c,d\}=\{a,b\}} x_I - \sum_{I: I\cap\{a,b,c,d\}=\{a,c\}} x_I.$$
            \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.052467+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4aa0507ec5e98f658d77615f85cd54b677de0b2480e6a3b45fe36aa9f4b45e24","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:kernel of arXiv:2607.12564, 'Power-law and log-periodic degree tails for a family of probability generating function eq'] Let $\varphi(t)=\mathbb{E} e^{-tW}$ and $y(t)=1-\varphi(t)$, so the Laplace fixed point reads
\begin{equation}
  y(\lambda t)=\lambda\,y(t)-p\,y(t)^2=y(t)\bigl(\lambda-p\,y(t)\bigr).
\end{equation}
Then, for every $m\in\mathbb{Z}$, with $z_m:=\rho+i\chi m$ and $C(z):=1/\Gamma(-z)$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}[W^{z_m}]=C(z_m)\int_0^\infty y(t)^2\,t^{-z_m-1}\,dt .
\end{equation}
Writing $t=\lambda^{v}$ and $H_p(v):=\lambda^{-\rho v}\,y(\lambda^{v})^{2}\ge0$, (equation eq:ysquare of the paper)
reads $\mathbb{E}[W^{z_m}]=C(z_m)\log\lambda\,\widehat H_p(m)$, $\widehat H_p(m)=\int_\mathbb{R} H_p(v)e^{-2\pi imv}dv$;
since $C(z_m)\ne0$, (cor:const of the paper) and (prop:reduce of the paper) show that, for $j\in\{1,2\}$, $\Psi_j$
is constant iff the periodization
$\mathcal P_p(u):=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}H_p(u+n)$ is constant. Moreover the unit-shift ratio of $H_p$,
\begin{equation}
  \frac{H_p(v+1)}{H_p(v)}=\lambda^{2-\rho}\Bigl(1-\tfrac{p}{\lambda}\,y(\lambda^{v})\Bigr)^{2}
