{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:22:01.678729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4cf36846d4007d484e06b43a2fbce24301a3d36ffa75e6c319cdb7434124fb9c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Question of arXiv:2605.02226, 'Finitely Dependent Processes on Subshifts'] \begin{enumerate}
\item A $d-$dimensional domino is a $ d$-dimensional box whose one side has length $2$ and all other sides have length 1 with its vertices in $\mathbb{Z}^d$. Does there exist a strongly irreducible subshift contained in the space of domino tilings of $\mathbb{R}^d$ for $d\geq 3$?
    \item In general, is there a natural characterization of the set of boxes $\mathcal{R}$ for which the space of tilings of $\mathbb{R}^d$ by the boxes in $\mathcal{R}$ contains a strongly irreducible subshift?

    We know that if the collection $ \mathcal{R}$ satisfies the condition of (thm:eventuallyuniversaltiles of the paper), then there exists a shift-invariant finitely dependent process on the tiling space $X(\mathcal{R})$. However, if $ p_1, p_2, \cdots, p_d$ are prime numbers and $ \mathcal{R} = \{ R_1, R_2, \cdots, R_d\}$ is a collection of bars where $ R_i$ is a bar whose sidelength in $ e_i$ direction is $ p_i$, then does $ X(\mathcal{R})$ contain a strongly irreducible subshift?
    \end{enumerate} (Stated as a question by the paper; open unless the paper says otherwise.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.661811+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4cf8e4a9da692750ccab7f88b3aeedbf685b56874a8c603de618061cb81d8290","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm DRC of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] Let $D$ be a Jordan domain and $D_\delta$ be as above. For the (critical) coupling $\mathbb P_\delta$, 
    \begin{equation}
        (h_\delta, \hat\mathbf{n}_\delta, \hat\mathbf{n}_\delta^\dagger, c_\delta, c_\delta^\dagger, \xi_\delta, \xi^\dagger_\delta) \overset{(d)}{\longrightarrow} (\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}\lambda}\ h, \hat\mathbf{n}, \hat\mathbf{n}^\dagger, c, c^\dagger, \xi, \xi^\dagger),
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
        \item h is a GFF with zero boundary conditions in $D$, 
        \item The inner boundaries of the boundary cluster of $\hat\mathbf{n}_\delta$ converge to $\mathcal L_{-\sqrt{2}\lambda, \sqrt{2}\lambda}$. The label $c(\ell)$ of an inner boundary $\ell$ is such that the boundary values of $h$ in $\mathcal O(\ell)$ are $c(\ell)\sqrt{2}\lambda$.
        \item The outer boundaries of the outermost clusters of $\hat\mathbf{n}^\dagger_\delta$ converge to $\mathcal L_{-2\lambda, 2\lambda}$. The sign $\xi^\dagger(\gamma)$ of an outer boundary $\gamma$ is such that the boundary values of $h$ in $\mathcal O(\gamma)$ are $\xi^\dagger(\gamma)2\lambda$. Moreover, the inner boundaries of the cluster corresponding to $\gamma$ converge to
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item [(iii.a)] $\mathcal L_{-2\lambda, (2\sqrt{2}-2)\lambda}$ if $\xi^\dagger(\gamma)=+1$,
            \item [(iii.b)] $\mathcal L_{-(2\sqrt{2}-2)\lambda, 2\lambda}$ if $\xi^\dagger(\gamma)=-1$,
            \item [(iii.c)] where the label $c^\dagger(\ell)$ of an inner boundary $\ell$ is such that the boundary values of $h$ in $\mathcal O(\ell)$ are $\xi(\gamma)(c(\ell)+1)\sqrt{2}\lambda$. 
        \end{enumerate}
        \item The structure repeats iteratively by applying the Markov property (MP-free of the paper) of Theorem (thm:master of the paper), which gives the nesting of two-valued sets described in Section (sec:decomp of the paper).
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.591159+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d253a5e00419dd641140a0ec1d382246db482b6d2b33452098acac2c1743775","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:productSpace of arXiv:2606.03079, 'Mean Field Limits for Stochastic, Underdamped Reactive Langevin Dynamics Models'] For reaction $\mathcal{R}_{\ell}$ with $\tilde{L}+1 \leq \ell \leq L$, i.e., having at least one product particle, define the product position and velocity product space analogously to $\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V}'^{(\ell)}$,
$$
\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V'}^{(\ell)}\in (\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d)^{|\beta^{(\ell)}|},
$$
where we write
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V}'^{(\ell)} & \supseteq (\bm{y},\bm{v'}) =\bigl((y_{1}^{(1)},{v'}_{1}^{(1)}), \cdots, (y_{\beta_{\ell 1}}^{(1)},{v'}_{\beta_{\ell 1}}^{(1)}), \cdots, (y_{1}^{(J)},{v'}_{1}^{(J)}), \cdots, (y_{\beta_{\ell J}}^{(J)},{v'}_{\beta_{\ell J}}^{(J)})\bigr).
\end{align*}

 For $(\bm{y},\bm{v'}) \in \mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V'}^{(\ell)}$ a sampled product position and velocity configuration for one individual $\mathcal{R}_{\ell}$ reaction, $y_{r}^{(j)}$ then labels the sampled position for the $r$th product particle of species $j$ involved in the reaction, and ${v'}_{r}^{(j)}$ labels the sampled velocity for the same product particle. Let $d \bm{y} d\bm{v'}=\bigl(\bigwedge_{j=1}^{J}(\bigwedge_{r=1}^{\beta_{\ell_{j}}} d y_{r}^{(j)}d {v'}_{r}^{(j)})\bigr)$ be the corresponding volume form on $\mathbb{Y}^{(\ell)} \otimes \mathbb{V}'^{(\ell)}$, which also naturally defines an associated Lebesgue measure.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.675819+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d47c631d7f4fa5fdea36c99cae6eac40b7cd08e601658255b0431257a2d60d2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 (Wendel's formula: Combinatorial version) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $n, d \ge 1$ be integers with $n \geq d+1$.
For every regular $X \in \R^{d\times n}$, the cardinality of the set $\mathcal M(X)$ is given by
\begin{align*}
    |\mathcal M(X)| = 2 \sum_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{n-1}{j}.
\end{align*}
In particular, this cardinality is the same for all regular $X$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.899644+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4d6399096595357676d0a5bff816d6a4849c190063623b57bcb0f181a0b79a4c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 6 (All-partition normalization) of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] Let $K$ be an oriented zero-framed knot.  For every $N\ge2$ and every
