\end{cases}
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.729279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6069dae4e423e82268cb4b94ce40f5f6d534ecf8975135b79b54e79dc76b144e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm_1 of arXiv:2503.22334, 'Probabilistic analysis of optimal multi-pivot QuickSort'] Let $K\in {\mathbb N}$ and $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$. For the normalization $Y_n:=(X_n-{\mathbb E}\left[X_n\right])/n$ as $n\to\infty$ we have 
\begin{align*}
   Y_n&\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow} Z_K,\\
   {\mathbb E}\left[\exp(\lambda Y_n)\right] &\longrightarrow {\mathbb E}\left[\exp(\lambda Z_K)\right]<\infty,
\end{align*}
where the distribution of $Z_K$ is determined as the unique centered, square integrable probability measure such that
\begin{align}
Z_K \overset{\text{d}}{=} \sum_{i=0}^{K} \kl*{D_i Z_K^{(i)}+\alpha_K D_i \ln\left(D_i\right)}+l_{\mathrm{opt}}(D),
\end{align}
where $D,Z_K^{(0)},\dots,Z_K^{(K)}$ are independent and $Z_K^{(i)}$ has the same distribution as $Z_K$ for all $i=0,\dots,K$.
Moreover, we have moments convergence, i.e., all moments of $Y_n$ converge to the moments of $Z_K$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:52:08.674161+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6072baee99818c0387fb0699d620decc9fb14fc20d7c4f1d1a8ae37dd01703ce","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 40 (Equivalence of intrinsic and fixed-chart norms) of arXiv:2608.30027, 'Under Ricci flow, a 3-torus goes flat'] Fix \(n\), \(m\geq2\), \(\sigma\in(0,1)\), \(\Lambda\geq1\), and \(L>0\).
Suppose that
\(\PBG_m(s;\Lambda;[a,b])\) holds and \(b-a\leq L\).  Let
\(\|\cdot\|_{X_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}\) and
\(\|\cdot\|_{Y_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}\) denote the suprema of the Euclidean
component norms in the fixed lifted atlas supplied by
Lemma~\ref{lem:uniform-parabolic-atlas}.  Then there is
\(C=C(n,m,\sigma,\Lambda,L)\) such that every deck-invariant tensor \(U\)
satisfies
\begin{align}
 C^{-1}\|U\|_{X_s^m[a,b]}
 &\leq \|U\|_{X_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}
 \leq C\|U\|_{X_s^m[a,b]},                                      \\
 C^{-1}\|U\|_{Y_s^m[a,b]}
 &\leq \|U\|_{Y_{s,{\mathrm{fc}}}^m[a,b]}
 \leq C\|U\|_{Y_s^m[a,b]}.                                      
\end{align}
The analogous comparison holds for every spatial
\(C^{k,\sigma}\)-norm with \(0\leq k\leq m\).  For tensors on the universal
cover that are not deck-invariant, the same local comparison holds with the
fixed-chart side interpreted as the uniformly local norm.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:04.961988+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_607a3d0e8ad8da71f6d2a696faf50255dbb7d99b9236e2244e5bf2e212958b7a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 (Potter bound at $0$) of arXiv:2604.00642, 'Quantitative central limit theorem for an integrated periodogram via the fourth moment the'] For any slowly varying $L$ at $0$ and any $\varepsilon>0$, there exist $C_\varepsilon$ and $\delta>0$ such that
