{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.614975+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6cfb48a9e457c1c165402afba692759cf78ec17037944c690c1d8c3a377813f6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 14 (Exit and entrance times) of arXiv:2506.09462, 'Transition Path Theory For L\'{e}vy-Type Processes: SDE Representation and Statistics'] Given a trajectory $\{X_t\}_{t \geq 0}$ starting from $x_0 \in A$, the last-exit-$A$-before-entrance-$B$ time $\tau_{A,n}^-$ and the first-entrance-$B$-after-exit-$A$ time $\tau_{B,n}^+$ belong to $\mathbb{T}$ if and only if
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            \lim_{t \uparrow \tau_{A,n}^-} X_t = x_n^A \in \bar{A}, \quad X_{\tau_{B,n}^+} \in \bar{B}, \quad \mbox{and}\quad X_s \notin A \cup B\quad
            \forall s \in (\tau_{A,n}^-, \tau_{B,n}^+).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
    Similarly, the last-exit-$B$-before-entrance-$A$ time $\tau_{B,n}^-$ and the first-entrance-$A$-after-exit-$B$ time $\tau_{A,n}^+$ belong to $\mathbb{T}$ if and only if
    \begin{equation*}
        \begin{aligned}
            \lim_{t \uparrow \tau_{B,n}^-} X_t = x_n^B \in \bar{B}, \quad X_{\tau_{A,n}^+} \in \bar{A}, \quad\mbox{and}\quad X_s \notin A \cup B \quad
            \forall s \in (\tau_{B,n}^-, \tau_{A,n}^+).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:48:47.286442+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6d06478cce4e1dd66bbe97d497c5ccc52586170dd094a3025faf990e4ace556c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:susyreplicas of arXiv:2604.20660, 'The Legendre structure of the TAP complexity\\ for the Ising spin glass'] Fix $n\geq 1$ and $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$. Set $\mathbf{m}^{(0)}=0$. Let $(u_i)_{1\leq i\leq n}$ and
		$(q_i)_{1\leq i\leq n}$ be strictly increasing sequences in $(0,1)$, and let
		$\zeta\in\mathrm{Prefix}_{n+1}\bigl((u_i),(q_i)\bigr)$ as in Definition~(def:nprefix of the paper).
		We define $\mathrm{SUSY}_n(\zeta,\varepsilon)$ as the set of
		$(\mathbf{m}^{(1)},\ldots,\mathbf{m}^{(n)})\in([-1+\delta_\varepsilon,1-\delta_\varepsilon]^N)^n$ where $\delta_\varepsilon:=\varepsilon^{\frac{1}{2}}$ and such that, for every $k\in[n]$
		and $j\in\{1,\ldots,k-1\}$,
		\begin{equation*}
			\left|\frac{1}{N}\|\mathbf{m}^{(k)}\|^2 - q_k\right| \leq \varepsilon
			\qquad\text{and}\qquad
			\left|\frac{1}{N}\langle \mathbf{m}^{(k)}-\mathbf{m}^{(k-1)},\mathbf{m}^{(j)}\rangle\right| \leq \varepsilon,
		\end{equation*}
		and, for every $i,k\in[n]$,
		\begin{equation}
			\left|\frac{1}{N}\langle\partial_m h_\zeta(q_k,\mathbf{m}^{(k)}),\mathbf{m}^{(i)}\rangle
			-\mathbb{E}\bigl[X_{q_k}\,\partial_x\Phi_\zeta(q_i,X_{q_i})\bigr]\right|\leq\varepsilon,
		\end{equation}
		and, for every $k\in[n]$,
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathrm{dist}\!\bigl(\zeta_{\mathbf{m}^{(k)}},\,\zeta|_{(q_k,1]}+\zeta([0,q_k])\,\delta_{q_k}\bigr)\leq\varepsilon.
		\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

