\item $(u^N,X^{1,N},V^{1,N},\dots,X^{N,N},V^{N,N})$ is measurable and adapted to the filtration $(\mathcal{F}_{t})_{t\geq 0}$.

\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:36:49.793460+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e37a2877bb501a4368bd9c1d90dc7305cde275f0dd3823bc06e5c96590bdc8f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main-nontriviality of arXiv:2609.02829, 'Quantitative explosion and percolation of the divisible sandpile'] Let $d\geq2$, let $(\mu_\rho)_{\rho\in(0,1]}$ be a family of laws with mean $\mathbb{E}_{\mu_\rho}\sigma(0)=\rho$, and let the masses be i.i.d.\ with law $\mu_\rho$. Suppose there are $\rho_0\in(0,1)$, $\nu_0>0$, $\theta_0>0$, and $K_0<\infty$ such that
