{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.242921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73f3cd37746d7d2676860a618c8e9d654c762faaf893b1310dcb62509ef09bc3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem reversible and tilted MC of arXiv:2604.23751, 'Large deviation principles for pattern-avoiding permutations, and limit shapes for constra'] Let $X$ be a finite state space, $q\ge1$, and $f:X\to\mathbb R_+$.
    Write $\mu^f$ for the $(q,f)$-tilted measure on $X$, that is, $\mu^{q,f}(x) \propto q^{f(x)}$ for all $x\in X$.
    If $p : X^2 \to [0,1]$ is a transition kernel, denote by $\tilde p$ the transition kernel characterized by:
    \begin{align*}
        \forall (x,y)\in X^2,\quad
        \tilde p(x,y) = 
        \left\{
        \begin{array}{ll}
            p(x,y) & \text{if }x\ne y \text{ and }f(y)\ge f(x) \,;\\
            p(x,y)q^{f(y)-f(x)} & \text{if }x\ne y \text{ and }f(y)< f(x) \,;
        \end{array}
        \right.
    \end{align*}
    and $\tilde p(x,x)$ is adjusted so that this defines a transition kernel.
    If $p$ is reversible for the uniform measure on $X$, then $\tilde p$ is reversible for the measure $\mu^{q,f}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:41:33.837877+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73f7e51b3406e3a827739436fc6090473f9f45a55a9e35e04b24043deb9614b7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 9 of arXiv:2604.11489, '2604.11489'] \cite{C-R}
For any random variables $X$, $Y$, real $x$ and constant $a>0$,
$$
\sup_{x}\big|\pp(X+Y\leq x)-\Phi(x)\big|\leq\sup_{x}\big|\pp(X\leq x)-\Phi(x)\big|+\frac{a}{\sqrt{2\pi}}+\pp\left(|Y|>a\right),
$$
where $\Phi(x)$ is the standard normal distribution. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:01:54.286158+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_740820b3f57a7fb85f08d08b47a187baebb79cfbffcd71ded895a80afa112c54","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem M of arXiv:2411.01847, 'Well-Posedness of Stochastic Chemotaxis System'] Suppose $h=(h_k)_{k=1}^\infty$, is an $\ell^2$-valued

progressively measurable function on $\Omega\times[0,T]\times\mathcal{O}$ such that $$\mathbb{E}\|\|h\|_{\ell^2}\|_{2,2,T}^2< \infty.$$
 Then $M(t)=\int_0^te^{-(t-s)A}h(s) \, dW_s$ is the solution to the following SPDE:
	\begin{equation*}
		\begin{cases}
dM(t) = -A M(t)  \, dt + h(t) \,  \, dW_t, \\
M(0) = 0,
\end{cases}
	\end{equation*}
	in $L^2(\mathcal{O})$, and has the $L^\infty$-regularity:
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{E} \sup_{t\in[0,T\wedge\tau]}\|M(t)\|_\infty^\eta\leq C(1+T)^{C'}\mathbb{E}\|\|h\|_{\ell^2}\|_{2r,2q,T\wedge\tau}^\eta.
	\end{align}
for any $\eta>0$, $r,q\in(1,\infty]$ with $\frac{1}{r}+\frac{1}{q}<1$ and stopping time $\tau$. The constants $C,C'$ may depend on $\eta, r, q $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:04:30.580073+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7416e1a7c8c2a7313cf0fa528b47267fb88ef5fdcdd9467e927be98c46f26c10","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:mixing of arXiv:2411.10333, 'Subcritical annulus crossing in spatial random graphs'] We say that \(\mathscr{G}\) is \emph{mixing} for \(\lambda>0\) if, for all \(|x|\ge 10\), we have 
  		\begin{equation*}\tag{\(\mathscr{M}\)}
  		 	\lim_{r\to\infty}\big|\operatorname{Cov}_\lambda\big(\1_{\mathcal{G}(r)}, \1_{\mathcal{G}(xr,r)}\big)\big| = 0.
  		\end{equation*}
  		We denote this property by \(\mathscr{M}_\lambda\). We say that the graph is \emph{uniformly mixing} for \(\lambda>0\), denoted by \(\overline{\mathscr{M}}_\lambda\), if, for any \(|x|\ge 10\), we have
  		\begin{equation*}\tag{\(\overline{\mathscr{M}}\)}	            \lim_{r\to\infty}\sup_{\lambda'<\lambda}\big|\operatorname{Cov}_{\lambda'}\big(\1_{\mathcal{G}(r)}, \1_{\mathcal{G}(xr,r)}\big)\big| = 0.
  		\end{equation*}
  		In some situations we require a polynomial decay of the correlations. If this is the case, we say that \(\mathscr{G}\) is \emph{polynomially mixing} for \(\lambda>0\) with exponent \(\xi=\xi_\lambda<0\) if, for any \(|x|\ge 10\), we have
  		\begin{equation*}\tag{\({{\mathscr{P}}\!\!{\mathscr{M}}}\)}
 		\limsup_{r\to\infty}\frac{\log\big|\operatorname{Cov}_{\lambda}\big(\1_{\mathcal{G}(r)}, \1_{\mathcal{G}(xr,r)}\big)\big|}{\log r}\leq \xi
 		\end{equation*}
 		and denote this property by \({{\mathscr{P}}\!\!{\mathscr{M}}}_\lambda^{\xi}\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.542067+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_741888fa356863a67d8f797ecde2bcc4bff41517c032006e47fef59ff089ddb7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2608.25971, 'Positive \texorpdfstring{$\tau$}{tau}-bi-Ricci curvature and Mean curvature flow with surg'] After choosing the normal so that $H\geq n\kappa$, a hypersurface satisfies the
\emph{quantitative $\tau$-bi-Ricci pinching condition} with constant
$\alpha_0>0$ if
\begin{equation}
 P_{ij}^{(\tau)}
 \geq\alpha_0 H|\lambda_p-\lambda_q|
 \qquad(i\ne j,\ p,q\text{ arbitrary}).
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.091210+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7420bfbd1e52a18cf4962965b9e4c388bdabd820a8e6581ffa9e2e3cbadcee33","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def1 definition:snakePath of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] Fix $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}}) \in \Theta$.  An element  $(\upvarrho , \omega) \in 
\mathbb{D}(\mathbb{R}, \mathcal{M}_f(\mathbb{R}_+) \times \mathcal{W}_{E})$  is called a snake path started from $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}})$ if the following properties hold:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\emph{(S1)}]   $(\upvarrho_0 , \omega_0) = (\mu,  {\mathrm{w}})$  and $\omega\in \mathcal{S}^\circ_{\mathrm{w}}$;
    \item[\emph{(S2)}] For every  $s \geq 0$, we have  $(\upvarrho_s, \omega_s) \in \Theta$; in  particular   $H(\upvarrho) = \zeta(\omega)$.
\end{enumerate}
The family of snake paths started from some fixed $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}}) \in \Theta$ is denoted by $\mathcal{S}_{\mu , {{\mathrm{w}}}}$, and when $(\mu , {\mathrm{w}})$ is of the form $(0,y)$ for some $y \in E$, we simply write $\mathcal{S}_{y}$. 
We set 
\begin{equation*}
   \mathcal{S} := \bigcup_{(\mu , {\mathrm{w}})\in \Theta}\mathcal{S}_{\mu , {\mathrm{w}}} 
\end{equation*}
for the collection of snake paths.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:01:01.831827+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_742bd7b2811cbab5ddf9c5d02ec45a976861232ca7c2b3eff2194fa7303d7b78","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:system-rewrite-detailed of arXiv:2609.00556, 'Higgs--Demailly System and Positivity of Higgs Bundles'] Let $h_t=e^{-f_t}g_t h_{\rm ref}$, where $g_t>0$ is
$h_{\rm ref}$-Hermitian and $\det g_t=1$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \sqrt{-1}\Lambda_\omega R_{D_{h_t}}
 =\left(\frac{\beta}{r}+\Delta_\omega^{\mathbb C} f_t\right)\operatorname{Id}_E
  +\sqrt{-1}\Lambda_\omega R^0_{D_{g_t h_{\rm ref}}}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the Higgs--Demailly system is equivalent to
\begin{equation}
 \left\{
 \begin{aligned}
  \det A_t&=e^{\lambda f_t}a_0,\\
  \sqrt{-1}\Lambda_\omega\left(
    R^0_{h_{\rm ref}}+\bar\partial(g_t^{-1}\partial^{h_{\rm ref}}g_t)
    +[\theta,g_t^{-1}\theta_{h_{\rm ref}}^*g_t]
  \right)&=-e^{f_t}\log g_t,
 \end{aligned}
 \right.
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
 A_t:=\left(\frac{\beta}{r}+\Delta_\omega^{\mathbb C} f_t+(1-t)\alpha\right)\operatorname{Id}_E
      -e^{f_t}\log g_t.
\end{equation}
Under this reformulation, admissibility is equivalent to $A_t>0$.","status":"unverified"}

