{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:48:27.276968+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_771bb2e5d09e8f1f1d0354e044f6f705733b1499d1a476ad3d5d1ba32db05eed","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2604.24664, 'Absolute continuity of Rosenblatt measures'] Let $\alpha \in (0, 1)$ and $f: [0, T] \to \Rbb$. The \emph{left} and \emph{right Riemann--Liouville fractional derivatives of $f$ of order $\alpha$} are defined by
	\begin{align*}
		(I^{-\alpha}_{0+} f)(x) &= \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \frac{\d{}}{\d{x}} \int_0^x (x-y)^{-\alpha} f(y) \d{y},
		\\
		(I^{-\alpha}_{T-} f)(x') &= \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \frac{\d{}}{\d{x'}} \int_{x'}^{T} (y-x')^{-\alpha} f(y) \d{y},
	\end{align*}
	for almost all $x,x' \in [0, T]$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.060390+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_771fe0d23b0ecd5c11811aebfdfe5223c450397e11ee9b69fddcc9604e57650f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thKM of arXiv:2607.14792, 'On the degree distribution of large tentacular Bienaymé--Galton--Watson trees'] Let $\eta$ be an offspring distribution such that $\eta(0) >0$ and $\eta(1) >0$ and denote by $F$ its generating function. Let $(S_n)_{n\geq0}$ be a random walk started from $S_0=0$ with step distribution $\eta$. Assume $k_n=o(n)$ and $k_n\rightarrow \infty$. Then
    \begin{equation}
