{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:53.446861+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_818d61cf76c77a70d848ed5d75238b6fc54a5769fc6f5df326bcd0bb455efb32","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2606.15843, 'Long-time behaviour of DLRA for SDEs'] Suppose  Assumptions~(H1)--(H6) hold. Moreover, suppose Assumption~\ref{AS:relation1} holds. 
    Then, there exists a constant $C(b,\sigma)>0$ such that for any $r\in\{1,2,\cdots,d\}$,
		\begin{equation}
			\sup_{t\in[0,\infty)}\bb E[|X^{(r)}_t|^2]\le C(b,\sigma)\big(1+\bb E[|X^{(r)}_0|^2]\big).
		\end{equation}
		In particular, for $r=d$ we have 
		$
