{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615412+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ba7084bbaf2245398c6ed6ae86535db6dd542b19cf62102acd3417246f3d457","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 4 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Given~$n\ge1$ and a sample~$\ell_{\tau_\varrho}$ on~$\T_n$ under~$P^\zero$, let~$Y_n$ denote the a.s.-unique maximizer of~$x\mapsto\ell_{\tau_\varrho}(x)$ on~$\BbbL_n$. Then~$\theta_n(Y_n)$ converges in law to a random variable~$U$ on~$[0,1]$ described as follows: Given a sample~$\{Z_{T_k}\}_{k\ge1}$ of the measures in Corollary~\ref{cor-1.3}, let~$K$ be the smallest~$k\ge1$ maximizing
\begin{equation}

k\mapsto \log Z_{T_k}\bigl([b^{-1},1]\bigr)-2k\log b+G_k
