{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.212987+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9167f8e69c4c0a2e472ca1934693003fa8494b3a89b116bfd478255ff20d5edf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem of arXiv:2506.09364, 'A collection of results relating the geometry of plane domains and the exit time of planar'] Suppose $D$ is a Jordan* domain with boundary given by a Jordan* curve $\hat C$, and with complementary Jordan* domain $\tilde D$. Define a new domain $U$ by $U:=D \cup \tilde D \cup V$, where $V$ is an open subset of $C$. Suppose there are closed sets $K \subset D, \tilde K \subset \tilde D$ with the following properties.

    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\sup\{E_a[T(K^c)^p] : a \in \tilde K\} < \infty$.
        \item $\sup\{E_a[T(\tilde K^c)^p] : a \in K\} < \infty$.
        \item $\inf\{P_a(T(U)<T(\tilde K^c)): a \in K\} > 0$ or $\inf\{P_a(T(U)<T(K^c)): a \in \tilde K\} > 0$.
    \end{itemize}

    Then $bh(U) = \min\{bh(D), bh(\tilde D)\}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:53:31.974868+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91a042a04ba66a5fb810aae82e97ac03789793acd97d51c90a3de6e84ca8cc7f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 8 of arXiv:2606.11142, 'Strong invariance principles for diffusions, Markov chains and their perturbations.'] (Theorem 1.3 from \cite{KozhinaThesis}). Fix $T > 0$ and let us define a time-grid $\mathbb{T}_N := \{t_i\}_{i=1}^N$ for $N \in \mathbb{N}$. Under assumptions A1, A2, AD3 there exist constants $C \ge 1$ and $c \in (0, 1]$ such that for all $0 < t_i < t_j \le T$ and all $((x, y), (x', y')) \in (\mathbb{R}^{2d})^2$:
\begin{equation*}
|(p^\epsilon_h - p_h)(t_i, t_j, (x, y), (x', y'))| \le C \bar{\Delta}_{\epsilon,\gamma,q} \, p_{c,K}(t_j - t_i, (x, y), (x', y')),
\end{equation*}
where $p_{\epsilon,h}$ and $p_h$ stand for the transition densities of the perturbed and original Euler schemes respectively, starting from $(x, y)$ at time $t_i$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:33:46.778320+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91b30ef2d34da8f4b8bad50fd31ba7557c56f30c44d6808c2b2c17c0fc271247","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2603.25615, 'Fourier dimension of Mandelbrot cascades on planar curves'] For $1 \leq p,q\leq \infty$ and $1\le j\le n$, we let
$$
S(p,q,j,n) = \left( \sum_{I \in \Q_j} \left( \sum_{J \in \Q_n , J \subset I} \nu_n(J)^q \right)^{p/q} \right)^{1/p}.
$$
Then, we have
\begin{equation}
\E (S(p,q,j,n)) \lesssim b^{-j\widetilde{\tau}(p) / p  - (n-j) \widetilde{\tau}(q)/q}. 
\end{equation}
Note that $\lim_{p \rightarrow \infty} \widetilde{\tau}(p)/p =\alpha_{\min}$. Thus, in \eqref{eq:E(S(p,q,j,n))}, we adopt the convention $\widetilde{\tau}(\infty) / \infty =\alpha_{\min}  $ if $p=\infty$ or $q=\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.498726+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91cf9f749d3d8ff68880db94b5d4225a20b742ec04b7fca7b799d848ab2ef3e2","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:defncLmet of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Fix $\rho\in\mathbb{R} \cup \{-\infty\}$. For a  continuous path $\pi:[s,t]\to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$, its \emph{length in $\mathcal{L}^\rho$} is
		\begin{equation}  
	\|\pi\|_{\mathcal{L}^\rho}=\inf_{k\in \mathbb{N}} \inf_{s=t_0<t_1<\cdots < t_k=t} \sum_{i=1}^k \mathcal{L}^\rho(\pi(t_{i-1}), t_{i-1}; \pi(t_i), t_i).
		\end{equation}
		A path $\pi$ is a \emph{geodesic} from $(\pi(s), s)$ to $(\pi(t), t)$ if $\|\pi\|_{\mathcal{L}^\rho} = \mathcal{L}^\rho(\pi(s),s; \pi(t),t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.657369+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91e710e3a7e6f781c56456b8f6588bfc27c0a21b0ad6a9ae643e691556416772","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 21 (Tree-graph inequality; Aizenman and Newman
\cite{AN84}) of arXiv:2607.24561, 'Range convergence and sharp one-arm asymptotics\\for the critical percolation cluster in h'] For all $m\ge1$ and \emph{all} $x_1,\dots,x_m\in\Z^d$ (not necessarily
distinct, and not necessarily distinct from $0$),
\begin{equation}
\tau_{m+1}(0,x_1,\dots,x_m)\;\le\;
\sum_{T\in\T_m}\ \sum_{(z_v)_{v\in V_{\mathrm{int}}(T)}\in(\Z^d)^{V_{\mathrm{int}}(T)}}
\ \prod_{\{a,b\}\in E(T)}\tau_2(w_a,w_b),
\end{equation}
where $w_0:=0$, $w_i:=x_i$ for leaves and $w_v:=z_v$ for internal vertices.
The bound holds verbatim when some of the points coincide: with the
convention that a repeated point is not repeated in the connection event,
$\tau_{m+1}(0,x_1,\dots,x_m)$ is then a connection function of the distinct
points among $0,x_1,\dots,x_m$, which is still dominated by the displayed
sum (the right-hand side only grows when leaves are placed at coinciding
positions). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-09T07:38:10+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_91f6917ae26f92fbd15a4ddb5a47350ce53715fe58f9cbe2aaf38043c418759f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 2.4 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $t>0$ and any $0<a\le b\le1$, the probability distribution $P^\varepsilon_c( \cdot \, : \, t,a,b)$ over $\mathbb N_0$ converges in law to a Poisson distribution with parameter 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
f(0) \, t\,  \lambda_* ( [a,b]) = f(0)\,  t \, \int_a^b \cfrac{dx}{x^2} = f(0) \, t \,  (a^{-1}- b^{-1}) \  . 
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Furthermore, we have that, for any $n\ge 0$, the function
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n^\varepsilon: r \in[0,\infty) \mapsto \mathbb P ( e_1^\varepsilon \ge r, N^\varepsilon_{(0,r]} ([a,b]) = n) \in [0,1]
\end{split}
\end{equation}
converges uniformly on any compact interval $[0,t]$, $t>0$, as $\varepsilon$ vanishes to 
\begin{equation}

\begin{split}
\Phi_n: r \in [0,\infty) \mapsto e^{-r f(0)} e^{-r \lambda_* ([a,b]} \frac{(r \lambda_* ([a,b]))^n}{n!} \in [0,1] \ .
\end{split}
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.701300+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9210534c20bc15f411cbcc9e09450beb5148ad3273a28e5d77e3377ea693682b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Sgn-irred of arXiv:2609.00672, 'Deciding superellipticity and computing the Weierstrass normal form'] Let $g\ge 2$ and $n\ge 2$ with $\mathcal S_{g,n}\ne\emptyset$, and put $m=m(g,n)$. Then

\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_{g,n}=\mathcal M(g,C_n,\mathbf C_{g,n}),
\end{equation}

and $\mathcal S_{g,n}$ is irreducible of dimension

\begin{equation}
\dim\mathcal S_{g,n}=
\begin{cases}
m-3, & n\mid m,\\
m-2, & n\nmid m.
