		In particular, $a_\theta\uparrow 1/2$ as $\theta\downarrow0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:46:36.095488+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9be00a4cdc94c09d62a8dd29c41fa3586b6b57086e8a0231d0fea7d9e8c28bd4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem12 of arXiv:2609.02795, 'Global smooth behavior in Kuznetsov and Westervelt type\\ viscous wave equations:\\ A unif'] Let $H^{(1)}$ and $H^{(2)}$ be as in Lemma (lem11 of the paper). Then
  \begin{eqnarray}\label{12.1}
	& & \hspace*{-20mm}
	\frac{d}{dt} \bigg\{ \frac{1}{2} \int_\Omega |\nabla u|^2
	+ \int_\Omega H^{(1)}(u,u_t) \bigg\} + a \int_\Omega |\nabla u|^2 \nonumber\\
	&=& \int_\Omega f(u,u_t,\nabla u,\nabla u_t) u
	+ \int_\Omega H^{(1)}_\xi(u,u_t) u_t
  \end{eqnarray}
  and
  \begin{eqnarray}\label{12.2}
	& & \hspace*{-20mm}
	\frac{d}{dt} \bigg\{ \int_\Omega H^{(2)}(u,u_t) + \frac{a}{2} \int_\Omega |\nabla u|^2 \bigg\}
	+ \int_\Omega |\nabla u_t|^2 \nonumber\\
	&=& \int_\Omega H^{(2)}_\xi(u,u_t) u_t
	+ \int_\Omega f(u,u_t,\nabla u,\nabla u_t) u_t
  \end{eqnarray}
  for all $t\in (0,T_{max})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.413628+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9be42f3e52c643c0a96d65eeaf5718ea1074d671b7b7589347685790b967eba7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq: first variation closed of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] For $p\in (1,\infty)$, the immersed curve $\gamma \in W^{2,p}(\R/\Z;\R^n)$ is called a \textit{closed $p$-elastica} if there exists $\lambda \in \R$ such that 
