{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.551584+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a060400e8813d794dbcfd0e6ced5cd1ba1ac97b07acef88bf229492a76a855f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] Let $X^n = §(x^{n}_1,\ldots, x^{n}_1)$ solve 
\begin{equation*}
\de x^{n}_i(t)=\int_{\Uptheta}G_{\mu^{n}(t)}(x_{i}^n(t);\bm{\theta})\mathsf{W}(\de \bm{\theta}, \de t),
\end{equation*}
with $\mu^n = \mu_{X^n}$. 
Let $(\bar x_1,\ldots,\bar x_n)$ be conditionally i.i.d., given $(\mathscr{F}^{\mathsf W}_t)_{t\geq0}$, copies of the unique solution $x$ of \eqref{eq:non_linear_SDE_common}, driven by the same common noise $\mathsf{W}$, and let $\bar\mu$ be their empirical measure. Then for every $T>0$ there exists $C_T>0$ such that 
\begin{align*}
    \max_{i\in[n]} \E \sup_{t\in[0, T]} \left\|x^{n}_i(t)-\bar x_i(t)\right\|^2 &+ \E \sup_{t\in[0, T]} W_2^2(\mu^n(t), \bar\mu^n(t)) \\
    &\leq  \frac{C_T}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \E \left\|x^{n,i}(0)-\bar x^i(0)\right\|^2.
\end{align*}
In particular, after choosing an optimal coupling of the initial data, the right hand side may be written as $C_T \E W_2^2(\mu^n(0), \bar \mu^n(0))$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.314443+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a06e0bc912f8c6e4f48f3db5ee743064f6cc6857c3117d8a95e15d2c657167f1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 48 of arXiv:2608.05931, 'Effective Lagrangian regularity and the uniqueness threshold for random H\"older velocity '] Suppose $u : \T^d \to \R^d$, $\alpha \in (0,1)$, and $Z := \{u =0\} \subseteq \T^d$ are such that
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $\nabla \cdot u=0,$
        \item $u \in C^\alpha(\T^d)$,
        \item $\liminf_{r \to 0} r^{-(1-\alpha)} |\{ x \in \T^d : d_{\T^d}(x,Z) \leq r\}| =0$,
        \item and for all $\tau>0, y \in \T^d$, if $X^1, X^2$ are solutions to~\eqref{eq:abstract ode} on $[0,\tau]$ such that for all $t \in [0,\tau]$, $X^1_t, X^2_t \not \in Z$, then $X^1|_{[0,\tau]} = X^2|_{[0,\tau]}$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then for almost every $y \in \T^d$,~\eqref{eq:abstract ode} admits a unique solution on $[0,\infty)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.075113+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a083ad505b9245976fb4266c711100599fb78a3c4a3acc3ee66cf52c44a9d5d4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2606.02036, 'Self-intersection local times for Volterra Gaussian processes in stochastic flows with int'] The stochastic differential equation
\begin{align}

\begin{cases}
\differential{x}(v,t)=a(x(v,t),\mu_t)\differential{t}+\int_{\mbR^d}b(x(v,t),\mu_t,z)W(\differential{t},\differential{z}), \\	
x(v,0)=v,\ v\in\mbR^d, \\
\mu_t=\mu_0\circ x(\cdot,t)^{-1},\ t\geqslant 0, 
\end{cases}
\end{align}
is said to be the equation with interaction.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.181201+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0c134691054c08d539ed86d1e01674e3b419f46fc3836e4bc9e09211215f5b9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 116 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mf A$ be a regular HTM, $I$ and $J$ be sets, and $K = I\times  J$. Let $\sm{\mf A}{k\in K}{f(k)}$ and $x = \sum_{k\in K} f(k)$.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item [(i)] Let $\sm{\mf A}{j\in  J}{f(i,j)}$ for every $i\in I$. Then $x = \sum_{i\in I}\sum_{j\in J}f(i,j)$.
  \item [(ii)] Let $\sm{\mf A}{i\in  I}{f(i,j)}$ for every $j\in J$. Then $x = \sum_{j\in J}\sum_{i\in I}f(i,j)$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.651057+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d55c9693e6a13dfd17af72b16cfc1575049983a1440d1439281284e89f35c8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2602.06011, 'Excursion decomposition of the XOR-Ising model'] A set $A$ is a thin for the field $\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}$ if for any continuous, bounded function $f:\mathbb C\to\mathbb R$, 
    \begin{equation*}
        \lim_{n\to\infty}(\field{e^{i\alpha\phi}}, f\1_{[A]_n})= 0,
    \end{equation*}
    where the limit is in probability. The same definition applies to the fields $:\! \cos(\alpha\phi) \!: $ and $:\! \sin(\alpha\phi) \!: $.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224361+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0d6a4e2238a3e2a0ef28002261d52c984fb6181857cd6f9bb166e0eee9a73aa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm:FractionalStab of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] There exists a positive constant $C = C (n,\gamma) > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
         \int_{\mathbb{R}^n } w (-\Delta )^{\gamma}w  dx - \frac{1}{S(n,\gamma) }\left( \int_{\mathbb{R}^n } |w|^{2^{*}} dx  \right)^{\frac{2}{2^{*}}} \geq C \text{dist}(w,\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n} )^2 \quad \forall w\in \dot{H}^{\gamma } (\mathbb{R}^n),
    \end{equation*}
    where
    \begin{align*}
        \text{dist}(w,\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n} ) := \inf \{\|w-u \|_{\dot{H}^{\gamma}(\mathbb{R}^n)} : u \in \mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n}  \},
    \end{align*}
    and $\mathcal{M}_{\mathbb{R}^n}$ is the set of extremal functions for the fractional Sobolev inequality. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.375889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0deff50edfc29d239731c1db0918d3446d69f3175e45310e5d9ca0759b611c7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:viscosity of arXiv:2412.01995, 'Exciting games and Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations'] The convex value function $v:[0,1) \times \Delta \to \mathbb R$ satisfies the dynamic programming principle, i.e., for any $(t,x) \in [0,1) \times \Delta $
    \begin{align*}
v(t,x)=&\textstyle  \inf_{\mathbb Q \in \mathcal{M}_{t,x}^{win}} \inf_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t^1} \mathbb E_{\mathbb Q}\left[ \int_{t}^{\tau} - \log \det \Sigma_u \, du + v(\tau,X_{\tau}) \right] \\
=& \textstyle \inf_{\mathbb Q \in \mathcal{M}_{t,x}^{win}} \sup_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t^1} \mathbb E_{\mathbb Q}\left[ \int_{t}^{\tau} - \log \det \Sigma_u \, du + v(\tau,X_{\tau}) \right],
    \end{align*}
    where $(X_t)_{t \in [0,1]}$ is the canonical process defined on the path space, and $\mathcal{T}_t^1$ denotes the set of stopping times with respect to the canonical filtration taking values in $[t,1]$. Moreover, the value function $v(t,x)$ is a viscosity solution to (equation eq:HJB of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.319949+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1341f00ae797767f86f3f0d56703efc221b89ef686b98a11142076336d31b29","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:integrate-one-spin of arXiv:2411.06195, 'Restrictions of some reinforced processes to subgraphs'] Let $1\in\Lambda$ and set $1^c=\Lambda\setminus\{1\}$.
  One has
  \begin{align}

    \int_{H^{2|2}}\mathcal{D}\sigma_1\, e^{\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\left\langle{\sigma_1,\sigma_i}\right\rangle}
    =e^{-\|\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\sigma_i\|},
  \end{align}
  and consequently, 
\begin{align}

  \int_{H^{2|2}}\mathcal{D}\sigma_1\,
    e^{\frac12\sum_{i,j\in\Lambda}W_{ij}(1+\left\langle{\sigma_i,\sigma_j}\right\rangle)}
      =& e^{\frac12\sum_{i,j\in 1^c}W_{ij}(1+\left\langle{\sigma_i,\sigma_j}\right\rangle)
    +\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}-\|\sum_{i\in 1^c}W_{1i}\sigma_i\|}.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:11:11.693217+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a15ec6093f22a55c5b5406ca6aae0dcab3396a85f326623f43e763f410736331","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2609.00645, 'Global persistence of nearly radial concentrated vortices in a bounded domain'] Let $\mathbf a_*\in D$ be a strict local minimum point of $H$, and choose
$\rho>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
  B_\rho(\mathbf a_*)\Subset D,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
  H(\mathbf b)>H(\mathbf a_*)
  \quad
  \bigl(
  \mathbf b\in\overline{B_\rho(\mathbf a_*)}
  \setminus\{\mathbf a_*\}
  \bigr).
\end{equation}
Fix $\kappa,M,R>0$.  Then there exist positive constants
$C$, $\eta_0$, and $\varepsilon_0$, depending only on
$D,\mathbf a_*,\rho,\kappa,M$, and $R$, such that the following holds.

For every $0<\varepsilon<\varepsilon_0$, let
$\mathbf a_\varepsilon\in B_\rho(\mathbf a_*)$, and let
$\omega_{\varepsilon,0}\in L^\infty(D)$ be nonnegative and satisfy
\[
  \int_D\omega_{\varepsilon,0}\,\mathrm d\mathbf x=\kappa,
  \qquad
  \|\omega_{\varepsilon,0}\|_\infty\leq M\varepsilon^{-2},
  \qquad
  \operatorname{supp}\omega_{\varepsilon,0}
  \subset B_{R\varepsilon}(\mathbf a_\varepsilon).
\]
Let $\omega_{\varepsilon,t}$, $t\in\mathbb R$, be the corresponding Yudovich
solution of (equation eq:euler-intro of the paper).  If
\begin{equation}
  \eta_{\varepsilon,0}
  :=\mathcal D(\omega_{\varepsilon,0})
  +\kappa^2[H(\mathbf a_\varepsilon)-H(\mathbf a_*)]
  +\varepsilon
  \leq\eta_0,
\end{equation}
then, for every $t\in\mathbb R$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathsf C(\omega_{\varepsilon,t})\subset B_\rho(\mathbf a_*)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal D(\omega_{\varepsilon,t})
  +\kappa^2\sup_{\mathbf b\in\mathsf C(\omega_{\varepsilon,t})}
  [H(\mathbf b)-H(\mathbf a_*)]
  \leq C\eta_{\varepsilon,0}.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.856804+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a173aca04a6ee6bc0051da898125b86499a5bd4a64a029bbf76b18c12c37faa2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:independence of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] Let $x\in (0,1)$. Then it holds that $\beta_x$ and $\beta^{\mathrm{pin},x}$ (as introduced in Definition (def:l_and_bb_pin of the paper)) are independent. Further, the integration formula
    \begin{align}
    	\mathbb{E}\left[ F(\beta) \psi (\beta_x) \right]
    	&= \int_{\mathbb{R}} \mathbb{E}\left[ F(\beta^{\mathrm{pin},x}+y l^x)\right] \psi (y) \, \mathcal{N}(0,x(1-x))(dy) \nonumber\\
    	&= \int_{\mathbb{R}} \mathbb{E}\left[ F(\beta+(y-\beta_x) l^x)\right] \psi (y) \, \mathcal{N}(0,x(1-x))(dy)
    \end{align}
    holds for any $\psi \in C_b(\mathbb{R})$ and any bounded Borel-measurable map $F \colon L \to \mathbb{R}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.116162+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a17b1757d4a6e424835ca5f9757a1e85cb2588640168675e14dcfba1c552417b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop PRO73 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Assume that the assumptions of Theorems~(THM61-2 of the paper) and (THM61 of the paper) are both satisfied, and that the transition density function of $X$ satisfies the two-sided sub-Gaussian estimate (equation eq:71 of the paper). Let $x\in E$ and $B\in \mathscr{B}^n(E)$ satisfy $\tilde{d}(x,B)>0$. Then
 \begin{equation}
     \lim_{t\downarrow 0}\frac{\mathbb{P}^x[\sigma_B\leq t]}{\exp\left\{-\gamma_1 t^{-\frac{1}{\beta -1}}\right\}}=\lim_{t\downarrow 0}\frac{\exp\left\{-\gamma_2 t^{-\frac{1}{\beta -1}}\right\}}{\mathbb{P}^x[\sigma_B\leq t]}=0
 \end{equation}
 for any constants $\gamma_1,\gamma_2>0$ such that
\[
    \gamma_1<c_2\cdot \widetilde{d}(x,B)^{\beta/(\beta-1)}\leq C_2\cdot \widetilde{d}(x,B)^{\beta/(\beta-1)}<\gamma_2.
\]
In particular, if $x_1,x_2\in E$ and $B_1,B_2\in \mathscr{B}^n(E)$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
    c_2\cdot \widetilde{d}(x_1,B_1)^{\beta/(\beta-1)}>C_2\cdot \widetilde{d}(x_2,B_2)^{\beta/(\beta-1)},
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{t\downarrow 0}\frac{\mathbb{P}^{x_1}[\sigma_{B_1}\leq t]}{\mathbb{P}^{x_2}[\sigma_{B_2}\leq t]}=0. 
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:10.807815+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a18aaf5d59e8afaa26db26f0e5315095e38f77556cf539cd77b56e3233bec600","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 15 of arXiv:2512.19517, 'Spiking and Resetting'] For any $s>0$ and $\eta>0$, we have that 
