converges, as $\varepsilon$ vanishes, to zero, in $\mathbb L^1$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.849568+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1afc15664c502ed23be88edc3ded2abdfabc24658a097041a5b5f5b7d807cc3","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition $U$-Functionals of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] A function $U: \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}) \to \mathbb{C}$ is called a \emph{$U$-functional} iff $U$ meets both of the following two conditions:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $U$ is \emph{ray-analytic}, i.e.\ for all $\varphi$, $\psi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})$ the function $\mathbb{R} \mathrel{\reflectbox{$\in$}} s \mapsto U(\varphi + s \psi) \in \mathbb{C}$
		is entire analytic and therefore extends to an entire function on $\mathbb{C}$.
		\item $U$ is \emph{uniformly bounded of exponential order 2}, i.e.\ there exist constants $A$, $B\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ and $p \in \mathbb{N}_{0}$ s.th.\ for all $z\in\mathbb{C}$, $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})$ it holds
		\begin{equation*}
			|U(z\varphi)| \leq A \exp(B|z|^2\|\varphi\|_{p}^2).
		\end{equation*}
	\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:29.002582+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a1f2b4934c8cf612a7c33c09129f7713e395bc5119b10ff57525ddf0eb07fa83","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop gen supp of arXiv:2608.25471, 'Exact Hausdorff measures and fine geometry of collisions of independent Markov processes'] Define
  \begin{align}
    I &\coloneqq \{s \in (0,\infty) \mid C_{s+\delta}-C_s>0\ \text{for every }\delta>0\},\\
    J &\coloneqq \{s \in (0,\infty) \mid C_{s+\delta}-C_{(s-\delta)\vee0}>0\ \text{for every }\delta>0\},\\
    Z &\coloneqq \{s \in (0,\infty) \mid \hat{X}_s\in\diag(M)\}.
  \end{align}
  Then $I\subseteq Z\subseteq J$, $\hat{P}_{\bm{\rho}}$-a.s.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.751194+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a2006cfc269e1fe5bddbb2a38c0562149fff8a6401e256b71ca365dbdc26ff30","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:sde_kin_dens of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let Assumptions (assump:hor of the paper) and (assum:sigma_0 of the paper) be in force. 
Setting
\begin{equation}
\beta(t,x) : = B x +  \left( 
\begin{matrix}
\beta_0(t,x)  \\
{\bf 0}_{(d - d_0) \times 1}
\end{matrix}
\right)  , \quad \sigma(t,x) : = \left( 
\begin{matrix}
\sigma_0 (t,x)  \\
{\bf 0}_{(d - d_0) \times 1}
\end{matrix}
\right), \qquad (t,x)\in(0,T)\times\mathbb{R}^d,
\end{equation}
with $\beta_0\in L^{\infty}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^d)$, we have:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] SDE$(\beta,\sigma)$ is weakly well-posed (see Definition (def:well_posed_lin of the paper)). For any $(s,x)\in [0,T)$, the law of the (unique in law) solution to SDE$(\beta,\sigma;s,\delta_x)$, at time $t\in (s,T)$, has a density $p(s,x; t, \cdot)$.

\item[(ii)] 
There exists $C>0$, depending only on $\| \sigma_0 \|_{L^{\infty}_{T}\mathcal{C}^{\alpha}_B(\mathbb{R}^d)}$, $\| \beta_0 \|_{L^{\infty}((0,T)\times \mathbb{R}^d)}$, $\lambda$, $T$ and $B$, such that
\begin{equation}
 |  \nabla^{m}_{x^0} p(s,x;t,y)  |  \leq C (t-s)^{-\frac{m}{2}} \, p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\lambda}}(t-s, x  , y).

\end{equation}

for any $0\leq s < t < T$, any $x,y\in\mathbb{R}^d$ and $m=0,1,2$. Here, $p_{\mathscr{K},\cdot}$ is the Gaussian density in (equation eq:kol_dens of the paper).

\item[(iii)] Furthermore, for any $s\in[0,T)$ and $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, the flow of time-marginal densities of the solution to SDE$(\beta,\sigma;s,\nu)$ is the unique (weak) solution to FP$(\beta,\sigma;s, \nu)$ (see Definition (def:sol_lin_fp of the paper)). In particular, for any $x\in\mathbb{R}^d$, $(p(s,x; t, \cdot))_{t\in(s,T)}$ is a density solution to FP$(\beta,\sigma;s, \delta_x)$.
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.798570+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a227182083faceaa5a779d2a3b0adee4cff30ee57d1b4e97b20dc94c87e46147","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo 28 of arXiv:2609.02261, 'Quantization and process'] For every  $F$ and $G$ in  
   ${\cal S} ( \R^{2n})$ and if $h$ is small enough, we have,

