{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:32:18.855202+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b308db83a6c89bbdf2fb1f3ad29101445ddbd0bc0c3be081a02fb9ba7174db63","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2609.03875, 'Measure theory without infinities'] Let $\mathcal{K}$, $\mathcal{Q}$, and $\mathcal{R}$ be such that $A\cap B\in \mathcal{R}$ for every $A\in \mathcal{K}$ and $B\in \mathcal{Q}$.
