{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:32:23.562324+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b4f560df601d5a733c0a4c6bd4e7b633b0b228850b313f8511cb023ee8e175cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3.3 of arXiv:2603.13873, '{Solvability of BSDEs with possibly unbounded stochastic coefficients \\ on a general weig'] For each $n\geq1$, let $\xi^n, \xi\in L_\tau^p(\rho_\cdot;{\mathbb R}^k)$, both $g^n$ and $g$ be the generator of BSDEs   satisfying assumptions (A:H4 of the paper) and (A:H5 of the paper), and $(Y_\cdot^n, Z_\cdot^n)$ and $(Y_\cdot, Z_\cdot)$ be the weighted $L^p$ solution of BSDE$(\xi^n,\tau,g^n)$ and BSDE$(\xi,\tau,g)$, respectively, in the space of $H_\tau^p(\rho_\cdot;{\mathbb R}^{k}\times{\mathbb R}^{k\times d})$. If
$$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[e^{p \int_{0}^{\tau}\rho_r{\rm d}r}|\xi^n-\xi|^p+\left(\int_0^\tau e^{\int_0^s\rho_r{\rm d}r}\left|g^n(s,Y_s,Z_s)-g(s,Y_s,Z_s)\right|{\rm d}s\right)^p\right]=0,$$
then
$$
\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\mathbb{E}\left[\sup_{s\in[0,\tau]}\left(e^{p \int_{0}^{s}\rho_r{\rm d}r}|Y_s^n-Y_s|^p\right)+\left(\int_0^{\tau} e^{2\int_0^s \rho_r{\rm d}r}|Z_s^n-Z_s|^2{\rm d}s\right)^{\frac{p}{2}}\right]=0.\vspace{0.2cm}
$$
\begin{proof}
For each $n\geq1$, let
$$\hat{Y}_\cdot^n:=Y_\cdot^n-Y_\cdot, \ \ \hat{Z}_\cdot^n:=Z_\cdot^n-Z_\cdot \ \ {\rm and}\ \ \hat{\xi}^n:=\xi^n-\xi.$$
Then, for each $n\geq1$, $(\hat{Y}_\cdot^n,\hat{Z}_\cdot^n)$ is a weighted $L^p$ solution of the following BSDE:
\begin{align*}
\hat{Y}_t^n=\hat{\xi}^n+\int_t^\tau \hat{g}^n(s,\hat{Y}_s^n,\hat{Z}_s^n){\rm d}s-\int_t^\tau\hat{Z}_s^n{\rm d}B_s, \ t\in[0,\tau],
\end{align*}
where the generator $\hat{g}^n$ is defined by
$$\hat{g}^n(t,y,z):=g^n(t,y+Y_t,z+Z_t)-g(t,Y_t,Z_t),
~~(t,y,z)\in[0,\tau]\times{\mathbb R}^k\times{\mathbb R}^{k\times{d}}.$$
It follows from (A:H4 of the paper) and (A:H5 of the paper) that for each $n\geq1$ and $(y,z)\in{\mathbb R}^k\times{\mathbb R}^{k\times{d}}$,
\begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left<\hat{y},\hat{g}^n(t,y,z)\right>&=\left<\hat{y},g^n(t,y+Y_t,z+Z_t)-g(t,Y_t,Z_t)\right>\\
&\leq \mu_t|y|+\nu_t|z|+|g^n(t,Y_t,Z_t)-g(t,Y_t,Z_t)|, \ \ t\in[0,\tau],
\end{split}
\end{align*}
which implies that for each $n\geq1$, the generator $\hat g^n$ satisfies assumption (A:A of the paper) with $\bar{p}:=p$, $\bar\mu_t:=\mu_t$, $\bar\nu_t:=\nu_t$, $\bar\rho_t:=\rho_t$ and $f_t:=|g^n(t,Y_t,Z_t)-g(t,Y_t,Z_t)|$. Then, it follows from (pro2.1 of the paper) with $r=t=0$ that there exists a constant $C_{p,\theta}>0$ depending only on $p$ and $\theta$ such that
\begin{align*}
\begin{split}
&\mathbb{E}\left[\sup_{s\in[0,\tau]}\left(e^{p \int_{0}^{s}\rho_r{\rm d}r}|Y_s^n-Y_s|^p\right)+\left(\int_0^{\tau} e^{2\int_0^s \rho_r{\rm d}r}|Z_s^n-Z_s|^2{\rm d}s\right)^{\frac{p}{2}}\right]\\
&\ \ \leq C_{p,\theta}\mathbb{E}\left[e^{p \int_{0}^{\tau}\rho_r{\rm d}r}|\xi^n-\xi|^p+\left(\int_0^\tau e^{\int_0^s\rho_r{\rm d}r}\left|g^n(s,Y_s,Z_s)-g(s,Y_s,Z_s)\right|{\rm d}s\right)^p\right].
\end{split}
\end{align*}
Thus, the desired assertion follows by sending $n$ to infinity in the last inequality.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.242172+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b5126f214ecefe1a94d14c1ee2a65979d7cce1f32128b3a8a0cc5bbab9cb353e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop max_action of arXiv:2604.23751, 'Large deviation principles for pattern-avoiding permutations, and limit shapes for constra'] For any $\beta\in \mathbb R$, the action $A_{\beta}$ has a unique minimizer $(\pi_1^{(\beta)}, \pi_2^{(\beta)})$ in $\mathcal{D}$. For $\beta \leq 0$, we have $\pi_1^{(\beta)}=\pi_2^{(\beta)}=\frac{1}{2}\Leb_{[0,1]}$. 
    For $\beta>0$, the densities of $\pi_1^{(\beta)}$ and $\pi_2^{(\beta)}$ on $[0,1]$ are given by
	\begin{equation}
	    \rho_1^{(\beta)}(x) := \frac{1}{1+\exp\left(\beta(\frac12-x) \right)}, 
        \quad \rho_2^{(\beta)}(x) := \frac{1}{1+\exp\left(\beta(x-\frac12) \right)}.
	\end{equation}
	In particular, both $\pi_1^{(\beta)}$ and $\pi_2^{(\beta)}$ have total mass $\frac{1}{2}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.853503+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b541fcc6c9dfee3706f4f45c65188470b34aae12e2481efb7e314691af232aaf","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2608.23462, '2608.23462'] Let $p \geq 2$, $x \in \R^{d_1}$ and $y \in \R^{d_2}$. Assume that Assumptions~\ref{assu:regularity} and~\ref{assu:epsilon} hold and let $(X_t,Y_t)_{t\ge 0}$ be the unique solution of \eqref{eq:fastslow0} with initial condition $(X_0,Y_0)=(x,y)$. If the fast variable is $p$-dissipative then, for every $T > 0$, there exists a constant $C \ge 0$ such that for all $T_0 \ge 0$
\[
\E \bigg[ \sup_{t \in [T_0,T_0+T]}|X_t -\mathbf{x}_{t-T_0}(X_{T_0})|^p \bigg] \le C \eps_{T_0}^{p/2}
\]
where, for $\tilde x \in \R^{d_1}$, $(\mathbf x_t(\tilde x))_{t \ge 0}$ denotes the solution of the averaged ODE \eqref{eq:averaged0}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

