{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:56.960843+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8659cc3acd1562b954123c04597e91c4f34332b43880453b02ed23c7deb054a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma E[weak-sl]_asymp of arXiv:2604.05921, 'Simplicity of random hypergraphs'] If $\forall e \in E: \delta_e^{\textnormal{tail}} = \delta^{\textnormal{tail}}\in O(1)$ and $\delta_e^{\textnormal{head}} = \delta^{\textnormal{head}}\in O(1)$ and 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathds{E}[(d_U^{\textnormal{out}})^{\delta^{\textnormal{tail}}}(d_U^{\textnormal{in}})^{\delta^{\textnormal{head}}}] \in o(n)$
    \item $\exists c>0 \textnormal{ s.t.}    \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \mathds{P}(d_U^{\textnormal{out}} \geq \delta^{\textnormal{tail}}), \mathds{P}(d_U^{\textnormal{in}} \geq \delta^{\textnormal{head}}) \geq c$
\end{enumerate}
then
\begin{align*}
    \mathds{E}[WS_n] &= \Big( \frac{\delta^{\textnormal{tail}} + \delta^{\textnormal{head}}-2}{2} + \frac{\delta^{\textnormal{head}} \mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{out}}d_U^{\textnormal{in}}]}{\mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{in}}]} \Big)(1+o(1)).
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:58.356574+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b865bf310e86eed77d482e740764d6cfcdde7d8c3e8f55d4574697267ab630d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2506.18558, '2506.18558'] {\rm(\textbf {Strong averaging principle})}
Suppose that Assumptions {\rm\ref{A1}}, {\rm\ref{A2}}, {\rm\ref{A3}} and {\rm\ref{A4}} hold. Then, for any initial value $(x,y)\in\RR^{n}\times\RR^{m}$ and $T>0$,
\begin{align}
\lim_{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0}\sup_{t\in[0,T]}\mathbb{E}|X_{t}^{\varepsilon}-\bar{X}_{t}|^2=0,
\end{align}
where $(\bar{X}_t)_{t\ge0}$ is the unique strong solution to the following averaged SDE \begin{equation}
d\bar{X}_{t}=\bar{b}(\bar{X}_t)\,dt+\bar\sigma(\bar{X}_t)\,d W^1_t,\quad\bar{X}_{0}=x.
\end{equation}
Here, $\bar{b}(x)=\displaystyle\int_{\RR^{m}}\hat b(x,y)\,\mu^x(dy)$, and $\mu^x$ is the unique invariant measure of the  SDE \eqref{e:IN}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:09.037454+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8663f2fae71f973cb85b6dd3fb78a875bd671ce7318816eb2dd410b408d4b45","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:mixing of arXiv:2608.25279, 'Provable Non-Acceleration of Standard Strang\\ Splittings of Kinetic Langevin Dynamics'] Assume the setting of Theorem~(thm:dichotomy of the paper).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\rm(i)] In case \emph{(Q)}, choose $\lambda\in[1,\kappa]$ for which (equation eq:quadrootrateintro of the paper) holds, and let $\pi_\lambda$ be the unique invariant law of $Q_{h,\gamma}^{U_\lambda}$. Then there are initial states $z,\widetilde z\in\mathbb R^4$ and a constant $c>0$ such that, for every $\varepsilon\in(0,c/4]$, there exists $z_\varepsilon\in\{z,\widetilde z\}$ satisfying
\begin{equation*}
 t_{\mathrm{mix}}(z_\varepsilon,\varepsilon;Q_{h,\gamma}^{U_\lambda},\pi_\lambda)
 \ \ge\ \frac{\kappa}{3C_\star}\,\log\frac{c}{4\varepsilon}-1.
\end{equation*}
\item[\rm(ii)] In case \emph{(C)}, for every $R\ge1$ and every $\varepsilon\in(0,3/8)$,
\begin{equation*}
 t_{\mathrm{mix}}\bigl(z_R,\varepsilon;Q_{h,\gamma}^{U_R},\pi_R\bigr)
 \ >\ \left\lfloor\tfrac1{16}e^{c_0R^2}\right\rfloor,
 \qquad
 W_2(\delta_{z_R},\pi_R)\le C_0(1+R).
\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:05:28.080682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b88212646f85c1e10618b3a7b3fb4293ff797cf0890838f3603c91f1ebd1db3e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem maintheorem of arXiv:2601.20502, 'Optimal matching under size priority'] Let $\pi$ be a  reproduction law and $\omega$ a distribution on $\mathbb{R}$ such that:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item both $\pi$ and $\omega$ have finite expectation,
        \item the law $\omega$ is atomless and admits a density on some open interval,
        \item denoting by $\phi$ the generating function of $\pi$ and setting $\hat{\phi}= \frac{\phi'}{\phi'(1)}$, the function
        \[t \mapsto \hat{\phi}(1-\hat{\phi}(1-t))\]
        has a finite number of fixed points.
    \end{itemize}
    Then there exists a unique (in law) unimodular optimal matching $(\mathbb{T},o,\mathbb{M}_{\mathrm{opt}})$ whose marginal tree $(\mathbb{T},{o})$ is a Bienaymé-Galton-Watson tree with reproduction law $\pi$ and i.i.d.~weights of law $\omega$. Furthermore the distribution of $(\mathbb{T},{o},\mathbb{M}_{\mathrm{opt}}(\mathbb{T}))$ is described in Proposition~(prop:Zconstruction of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:25:40.387468+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8d1f55a9b21c0ecac05acf312102587c7d9524aa3747258d4b51b2a97d62072","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:density of arXiv:2503.21737, 'High-intensity Voronoi percolation on manifolds'] Let $\delta, C>0$. There exists $r_0 = r_0(\delta,C)>0$ such that for $r\geq r_0$, $\lambda>0$ and $\xi\sim \mathrm{PPP}\big(\lambda dx, \mathbb R^d\big)$ we have
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P \bigg(\mathrm{d}_{\mathbb{R}^d}(x,\xi)\leq r\lambda^{-1/d}/C \; \text{ for all } x\in B_{\mathbb R^d}\big(0, r\lambda^{-1/d}\big) \bigg) \geq 1-\delta.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.327932+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b8d72f25d8398fdac6e01d0c574df1c3d5998bc8e4dc722cda60df767772cdcd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:heat_content_large of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Assume that $\phi$ satisfies (equation eq:Bernstein_cond of the paper) and $\Omega\subset\mathbb{G}$ is a bounded, open, connected subset. Then the first eigenvalue $\lambda_{1,\phi}$ of $\Delta^{\phi,\Omega}_{\mathcal H}$ is simple and admits an eigenfunction $f_\phi$ such that $f_\phi(x)>0$ for all $x\in\Omega$, and $\|f_\phi\|_{L^2(\mathbb G, dx)}=1$. Moreover, 
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{t\to \infty} e^{t\lambda_{1,\phi}}\widetilde{H}^\phi_\Omega(t)=\langle f_\phi,\mathbbm{1}_\Omega\rangle^2_{L^2(\Omega,dx)}.
