{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.332121+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd3ab78c52705ab89930dd41738e23d18bcec37c53253ce57f9b92548ebb4b11","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:form_core_2 of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Let $\Omega$ be an open subset of $\mathbb G$. Then, $((\mathcal{E}^\phi)^\Omega, (\mathcal{F}^\phi)^\Omega)$ is a regular Dirichlet form on $L^2(\Omega,dx)$. Moreover, $C^\infty_c(\Omega)$ is a form core for $\mathcal{E}^{\phi,\Omega}$. As a result, the generator associated with $\mathcal{E}^{\phi,\Omega}$ is the Friedrichs extension of $(\mathcal{L}^\phi_\Omega, C^\infty_c(\Omega))$ in $L^2(\Omega, dx)$, where for any $f\in C^\infty_c(\Omega)$,
    \begin{align*}
        \mathcal{L}^\phi_\Omega f = \left.\Delta^\phi_\Omega f^0\right|_{\Omega},
    \end{align*}
    with 
    \begin{align*}
        f^0(x)=\begin{cases}
            f(x) & \mbox{if $x\in\Omega$} \\ 
            0 & \mbox{if $x\in\Omega^c$}.
        \end{cases}
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:02:34.840366+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd48ca07e5c3a0675d84f162196b534b1d3f00399de6ace87a53fa1de0a48392","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2607.20714, 'High Minima of Gaussian Processes: Overshoots and Minimizer Locations'] Suppose that $\sigma_*^2>0$, and let $T$ be any measurable selection from
$\argmin_{t\in K}X(t)$.  Then $M\in\mathbb D^{1,2}$ and
\begin{equation}
 DM=R_T:=R(T,\cdot)\qquad\text{almost surely}.

\end{equation}
The random element $R_T\in\cH$ is independent of the chosen minimizer
selection.  Moreover, if $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ is locally absolutely
continuous and $f'\in L^\infty(\mathbb R)$, then
$f(M)\in\mathbb D^{1,2}$ and, for every $h\in\cH$,
\begin{equation}
 \Ee\bigl\{W(h)f(M)\bigr\}
 =\Ee\bigl\{f'(M)h(T)\bigr\}.

\end{equation}
Here $f'$ may be chosen arbitrarily on its Lebesgue-null set of
non-differentiability.","status":"unverified"}

