{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.867654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd6a3883c0a0bec97de3e57b6b0467df295c3c4a99867444fdbcd4cb31020437","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 (Weak sub-solution) of arXiv:2608.26834, 'Small-time annealed large deviations principle for one-dimensional diffusions in a random '] A nonnegative function $u$ on $(0,S)\times D$ is called a bounded weak sub-solution  of the differential equation
		\begin{equation}
			\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}={\mathcal L}_W u
		\end{equation}
		if
		\[
		u\in L^\infty((0,S)\times D) \cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}((0,S);H^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(D)),
		\]
		and, for every nonnegative $\varphi\in C_c^1((0,S);H_0^1(D)\cap L^\infty(D))$, one has
		\begin{equation*}
			-\int_0^S\!\int_Du\,\partial_s\varphi\,\dd \mu_W\dd s
			+\int_0^S\mathcal E_W(u(s),\varphi(s))\,\dd s\le0.
		\end{equation*}
		If equality holds for all such $\varphi$, then $u$ is called a weak solution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-09T07:38:10+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cd7f42fa6cce70728b6f738f882eb1c9265b6470f11903f06083059a131ed96a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4.5 of arXiv:2507.06186, '2507.06186'] For any $\ka,\eps,t>0$, we let $\mc K_{\ka,\eps}(t)$ denote the integral operator on $L^2(D)$ with kernel
