{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:41:19.327698+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_cdfe90f5ad2cf8011284e88ecbda83bb6dfd6cba69bd7fe897b2496ae545ccc8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:sub-Laplacian of arXiv:2609.04045, 'Heat content and spectrum for subordinated sub-Laplacians'] Let $\{X_1,\ldots,X_{m}\}$ be an orthonormal frame for $\mathcal{D}$. Define the \emph{horizontal gradient} of $f$, denoted by
$\nabla_{\mathcal{H}}f$, as the horizontal section
\begin{equation*}
\nabla_{\mathcal{H}}f:=\sum_{i=1}^{m}(X_if)X_i,
\end{equation*}
for any function $f:{\mathbb G}\longrightarrow {\mathbb R}$ for which the partial derivatives
$X_if$ exist. The second-order differential operator 
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\mathcal{H}}:=-\sum_{i=1}^{m} X_i^2
\end{equation}
defined on $C^{\infty}(\mathbb{G})$ is called a \emph{sub-Laplacian}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.837366+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce0eeb5d8328a18151438e1e11572a07a8cae04a09d1d0e60d9c2eec452bfcde","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 36 of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] The following conditions are equivalent:
\item[(i)] $F$ is demicontinuous;
\item[(ii)] $F$ embeds isometrically as an order dense sublattice of a monotonically complete Banach lattice;
\item[(iii)] $F$ embeds isomorphically as a regular sublattice of a monotonically complete Banach lattice.\medskip

Moreover, the monotonically complete Banach lattice which contains $F$ isomorphically as an order dense sublattice is unique up to an isomorphism.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:33:06.543116+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce14a12171c62235d2c52faadd62e4f00e010a843a10579e7a5cefd7a05bfe66","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 18 (Higher derivatives) of arXiv:2608.25971, 'Positive \texorpdfstring{$\tau$}{tau}-bi-Ricci curvature and Mean curvature flow with surg'] For every pair of nonnegative integers $a,b$, there exists
