{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:06.329235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce61289fea7beb353dc54d76f3c980d6355d53951399e3c3f7d1e9cf6a32858a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 10 of arXiv:2607.06428, 'Impact of spinning on the early-warning signs in non-Markovian stochastic systems'] Let $H\in(0,1)$, $\tau\ge0$ and $(x_t)_{t\geq 0}$ be a solution to \eqref{eq:fast system simplified} in the setting \nameref{S1}. Then, we obtain that 
\begin{align}
    V_\infty(\tau)=& \frac{\sigma^2}{2}H|A(\lambda)|^{-2H}\\
    &\times \left( e^{A(\lambda)\tau}\Gamma(2H) + e^{-A(\lambda)\tau}\Gamma(2H,|A(\lambda)|\tau)-e^{A(\lambda)\tau} \int_0^{|A(\lambda)|\tau} e^w w^{2H-1}~\txtd w \right). \nonumber
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.859373+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce6aeab2f7def6360399f0adf52ee26574e53ac2c7c1d4b9f6f4ff30a5a35156","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor pers-det-trees of arXiv:2410.24170, 'Persistent hubs in CMJ branching processes with independent increments and preferential at'] Suppose that $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ is a generalised preferential attachment tree, with deterministic values $(f(j))_{j \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$. Then
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{f(j)^2} = \infty$, then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ almost surely does not have a persistent hub.
\item  If $\sum_{j=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{f(j)^2} < \infty$ and for some $\lambda > 0$, 
\begin{equation} 
\sum_{i=0}^{\infty} \prod_{j=0}^{i}\frac{f(j)}{f(j) + \lambda} < \infty,
\end{equation} 
then $(\mathcal{T}_{i})_{i \in \mathbb{N}_{0}}$ has a unique persistent hub almost surely. 
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:51:00.243742+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ce81fb8f5d5c071831d86ab0d22e74e6e15fd581348cf707a95db828280dc5ae","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:second of arXiv:2604.23791, 'Finite-sample Borel--Cantelli inequalities under mixing conditions'] Under the hypotheses of Theorem~(thm:phi of the paper) with $L\ge2$,
