{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:45:02.064779+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d240573f80621a8002f161ee703c9d6f99bc646096e6fccbb1b12104522e7967","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem cases_sb of arXiv:2601.00119, 'The largest common subtree of two random trees'] Let $h\geq 1$. If $\operatorname{LCS}^\bullet(\tau_*,\tau_*') > h+1$ then, for any $0<\alpha<1$, one of the following situations happens: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item[\emph{(i)}] There is $v\in \tau_*$ and $v'\in \tau_*'$ with $|v|=|v'|=1$ such that $\mathrm{Ht}(\theta_v\tau_*)\wedge \mathrm{Ht}(\theta_{v'}\tau_*')\le h$ and \[h^{-\alpha}\operatorname{LCS}^\bullet(\theta_v\tau_*,\theta_{v'}\tau_*')> \mathrm{Ht}(\theta_v\tau_*)\wedge \mathrm{Ht}(\theta_{v'}\tau_*') +1.\] 
        \item[\emph{(ii)}] It holds that 
        \[\left(\sum_{i=1}^{\mathrm{k}_\varnothing(\tau_*)}1+\mathrm{Ht}(\theta_i\tau_*)\right)\wedge \left(\sum_{i=1}^{\mathrm{k}_\varnothing(\tau_*')}1+\mathrm{Ht}(\theta_i\tau_*')\right)>h^{1-\alpha}.\]
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.368358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d26863b9872bc8c4c8d97687caabfc3cfe991286e43f45647339208ca802d989","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition thm:LSI:wu of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Let $\varphi \in C^2(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)$. Define the differential operator $\mathcal{L}_\varphi := \Delta - \nabla \varphi \cdot \nabla$. Let  $\mathcal{L}_\varphi^{-1}$ denote the Poisson operator, which is the inverse of $\mathcal{L}_\varphi$ on the Banach space of Lipschitz continuous functions $C_{\text{\normalfont Lip}, 0}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)$. That is, for $f \in C_{\text{\normalfont Lip}, 0}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)$, $h = \mathcal{L}_\varphi^{-1} f$ if $\mathcal{L}_{\varphi} h = f$. Recall the definition of $w(r, \varphi)$ in (equation eq:LSI:w of the paper). If
    \begin{align*}
        \hat{c}(\varphi) := \frac{1}{4} \int_0^\infty s \exp\bigg(\int_0^s \frac{1}{4}w(r, \phi) dr \bigg) ds < \infty,
    \end{align*}
    then $\mathcal{L}_\varphi^{-1}$ is a bounded operator on $C_{\text{\normalfont Lip}, 0}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^d)$ and we have $\|\mathcal{L}_\varphi^{-1} \|_{\text{\normalfont Lip}} < \hat{c}(\varphi).$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.527581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d26ca6f74131a42a2c004d7c05e197ce2a7445ed8b254910ef02354f438c87e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 (\cite{namba74}, see also Section 2.1 in \cite{Streets-Ustinovskiy21}) of arXiv:2608.30638, 'On compact steady pluriclosed soliton surfaces'] Let $(M,J)$ be a primary Hopf surface of class $1$, and we work over the open subset $\{z_1z_2\neq 0\}$. Consider the logarithmic coordinates
\begin{equation}
    w_j=x_j+\sqrt{-1}y_j=\log z_j.
