{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.617982+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5f7394502e4a0beded6a61dc9091545d6cf3c28ae41d042e12db81e0a0f1f09","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 34 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] There exists~$\tilde c>0$ such that for all $n\ge1$, all $\gamma\colon\{0,\dots,n\}\to[0,\infty)$ and all $s\ge u\ge0$,
\begin{equation}

\begin{aligned}
Q_{n,s,u}\Biggl(\biggl(\,\bigcap_{j=k}^{n-k'}\Bigl\{h(x_j)\le&\frac {n-j}n\wt m_n+s-\gamma(j)\Bigr\}\biggr)^\cc\,\cap\,\bigcap_{i=1}^n\Bigl\{h(x_i)\le\frac {n-i}n\wt m_n+s+\gamma(i)\Bigr\}\Biggr)
\\
&\le
\tilde c\frac{(1+s-u)(1+s)}n\sum_{\ell=k}^{n-k'}\frac{n^{3/2}}{[\ell(n-\ell)]^{3/2}}\,\gamma(\ell)^5
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
for any~$k,k'=1,\dots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ such that $\gamma(i)\ge1$ for all~$i=k,\dots,n-k'$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.255597+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_d5f9bb71d80d744cc22143a6a8b0d83cab3473afc6e11a0468d602ac3d89dd44","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 10 of arXiv:2609.01101, '2609.01101'] For $N\ge1$ and arbitrary complex numbers $d_n$,
