\end{equation*}
where $(b_i)_{i \in [k]}$ is the canonical basis of $\mathbb{R}^k$ and $\boldsymbol{1} = (1,\ldots,1)^T \in \mathbb{R}^k$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:27:56.777263+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_e1c08687c9e86bb8dbcb44c6a2a631cda12484cb1caa69c3bb533b0196e2375b","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 (Convexity of the negative particle-number current) of arXiv:2608.31171, '\bf Finite-energy spacetimes with torus symmetry. \\ \Large Cauchy stability of Einstein--'] Under the pressure hypotheses~\eqref{hyperbolic-eos-zero}, every state in the future causal cone is uniquely recovered from $Z_{\rm N}$, and the map
