{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:34:20.739716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f86942a2c59c0a606883139ae4499f90a8b556dcf19959879c9efff0d63ccfe6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 28 of arXiv:2609.02197, '2609.02197'] Let $\theta \in \left]-\infty,2-\frac{d}{2}\right[.$
\begin{enumerate}
\item
There exists a stochastic process $\left(\widetilde{X}_{r,1}\right)_{r \in \mathbb{R}_+}$ with state space $\left(\mathscr{C}^\theta,\mathcal{B}\left(\mathscr{C}^\theta\right)\right)$ such that the sample paths of $\left(\widetilde{X}_{q,1}\right)_{q \in \mathbb{R}_+}$ are continuous and for $\mathds{P}$-almost every $\omega \in \Omega,\left(\widetilde{X}_{r,1}\left(\omega\right)\right)\left(\varphi\right)=\left(X_{r,1}\left(\varphi\right)\right)\left(\omega\right).$
\item For all $\left(r,q\right) \in \mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}_+^*,$ let \gls{sto3}$:=\zeta_q*\widetilde{X}_{r,1}.$ For every $\left(U,\varsigma\right) \in \mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{R}_+^*,$ $$\lim_{\epsilon\downarrow 0}\mathds{P}\left(\sup_{r \in \left[0,U\right]}\left\Vert \widetilde{X}_{r,\epsilon,1}-\widetilde{X}_{r,1}\right\Vert_{\mathscr{C}^\theta}>\varsigma\right)=0.$$
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.094339+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f882633f5edf11c65d8fa93780f140c709f0ffd1b71cdfa9f8d2e07147509aaa","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop proposition:L* of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] Under $\mathbf{N}_{x,r}$, the following convergence  holds     \begin{equation} 
        \lim_{k\to\infty} \sup_{t\geq 0}\Big|L_{t}(\rho,W)- \frac{1}{\varepsilon_k} \int_0^t {\mathrm{d}} s \,  \mathbbm{1}_{\{ \uptau(W_s) <H(\rho_s) < \uptau(W_s) + \varepsilon_k \}} \Big|=0, \quad \text{a.e.} 
    \end{equation}
    Furthermore, if under $\mathbf{N}_{x,r}$  we write $(s_i,t_i)_{i\in \mathcal{I}}$ for the connected components of $\{H(\rho_s)>\uptau(W_s):~s\geq 0\}$ and we let $(\rho^i,\overline{W}^i)_{i \in \mathcal{I}}$ be the associated subtrajectories, conditionally on $L_\sigma(\rho,W)$,  the point measure 
         \begin{equation}
             \sum_{i \in \mathcal{I}}\delta_{(L_{s_i}(\rho,W), \rho^i, \overline{W}^i )}
         \end{equation}
        is a Poisson point measure with intensity $\mathbbm{1}_{[0,L_\sigma(\rho,W)]}(\ell) {\mathrm{d}} \ell \,  \mathbb{N}_{x,0}({\mathrm{d}} \rho , {\mathrm{d}} \overline{W})$, independent of $\mathrm{tr}(\rho,W)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.114241+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f891ca5e7721f79eaeb2edcd868330e884cf9800000b1f780b70bf12f80b5e6e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem LM24 of arXiv:2608.16170, '\bf On hitting time distributions of Markov processes with sub-Gaussian heat kernel bounds'] Let $x\in E$ and $B\in \mathscr{B}^n(E)$. Then the following statements hold:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(1)] If $B$ is finely open (in particular, if $B$ is open), then $d(x,B)=\widetilde{d}(x,B)$. 
    \item[(2)] If $B\subset \overline{\mathrm{int}^f(B)}$, then $d(x,B)=\widetilde{d}(x,B)$. In particular, if $B$ is the closure of an open subset of $E$, then $d(x,B)=\widetilde{d}(x,B)$. 
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.875380+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f89e60428e275221b8bfa26df96acbf9bb37928e9b7700ac4bb9d774e2f49856","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem genbb of arXiv:2608.27939, 'Entropy and domination for quasi-Hitchin representations'] Let $P,Q$ be distinct plaques, with separating sides $g_P\subset\partial P$ and $g_Q\subset\partial Q$ oriented parallel.  The change of canonical snake basis of $g_P$ to that of a side $g_Q^\prime$ of $Q$ is given by a matrix 
