{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.143147+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9cef7f65ebbfaba43e66d35ef5cfd0a7d33be621da4950ef7e86057ae3b13cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:constant-overshoot of arXiv:2607.04620, 'The Exact Ville Identity\\ \large From the Absorbing Case to the General Law\\ with an App'] Suppose there is a constant $\omega\ge0$ such that, on $\{T_b<\infty\}$, the conditional landing value obeys
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}[M_{T_b}\mid \mathcal{F}_{T_b-1},T_b=n]\le b+\omega
  \quad\text{a.s., for every }n.

\end{equation}
Then $O_b\le\omega$ and $\mathbb{P}(T_b<\infty)\ge(1-D_b-R_b)/(b+\omega)$, with equality throughout when (equation eq:landing-bound of the paper) holds with equality. In particular, if the conditional overshoot is a level-independent constant $\omega$, then $O_b=\omega$ and $\mathbb{P}(T_b<\infty)=(1-D_b-R_b)/(b+\omega)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:38:25.224752+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9d7efca4e88d21eca4165a219994c4d2799103b8f983a624f22ef3e8eb09cd2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Variationalppties of arXiv:2609.00324, 'Quantitative Stability for Fractional Yamabe minimizers'] For every $u \in \mathcal{B}$, the first variation of $I_{\gamma}$ on $\mathcal{B}$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{2} \nabla_{\mathcal{B}} I_{\gamma} [u] (\phi) = \int_M \phi P_\gamma^{\hat{h}}(u) d\sigma_{\hat{h}},
    \end{align*}
    for every $\phi \in H^\gamma (M) $. The second variation over $\mathcal{B}$ is given by
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{2} \nabla_{\mathcal{B}}^2 I_{\gamma} [u](\phi, \psi) = \int_M \phi P_\gamma^{\hat{h}}(\psi) d\sigma_{\hat{h}} - (2^* - 1)I_\gamma [u] \int_M u^{\frac{4\gamma}{n-2\gamma}}\phi \psi d\sigma_{\hat{h}}.
    \end{align*}
    for every $\phi,\psi \in H^{\gamma}(M) $. Moreover, the following properties hold
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item For $\alpha \in (0,1)$ such that $2\gamma + \alpha \notin \mathbb{N}$, the map $u \mapsto \frac{\nabla_{\mathcal{B}}^2 I_\gamma [u](\phi,-)}{\|\phi\|_{C^{2\gamma + \alpha}} }$ is locally continuous from $C^{2\gamma + \alpha}(M) \cap \mathcal{B} \to C^{ \alpha}(M)$ with a modulus of continuity uniform over $\phi\in C^{2\gamma + \alpha}(M)$.
        \item The function $u \mapsto \frac{\nabla_{\mathcal{B}}^2 I_\gamma [u](\phi,\psi)}{\|\phi\|_{H^\gamma } \|\psi\|_{H^\gamma  }}$ is a locally continuous function from $ \mathcal{B} \to \mathbb{R}$ with a modulus of continuity uniform over $\phi,\psi \in H^\gamma (M)$.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.824915+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9f61df9bd281d3627bfd40a7dc39be8b3ae2e1da1690faa1957b0f9870cf13e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 31 of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] The following conditions are equivalent:
\item[(i)] $F$ is $r$-monotonically complete;
\item[(ii)] Every directed dominable subset of $\Ba_{F_{+}}$ has a supremum in $F$ of norm at most $r$;
\item[(iii)] $F$ is norm complete and every disjoint $D\subset F_{+}$ such that $D^{\vee}\subset\Ba_{F}$ has a supremum of norm at most $r$;
\item[(iv)] The order adherence of $\Ba_{F}$ in $F^{u}$ is contained in $r\Ba_{F}$;
\item[(v)] Every uo-Cauchy net in $\Ba_{F}$ has a uo-limit in $r\Ba_{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.586120+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_f9fd27db0ee2782c89c12c6a678dba0423a5b4507cb981613a94c1133c2b49e1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm convnex_entropy of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] Let $\Pi_i\in \mathcal P_{s,1}( \mathsf{S})$, $i=0,1$ and assume that $\mathscr  W_{gap,p}(\Pi_0,\Pi_1)<\infty$ for $p>1$. Then, there exists an optimal coupling  $\mathsf U\in  \mathsf{Cpl}_s(\Pi_0,\Pi_1)$, such that for any $0\leq t\leq 1$ \begin{align}
       \mathcal E^*((T_t)_{\#}\mathsf U) \leq (1-t) \mathcal E^*(\Pi_0)+t\mathcal E^*(\Pi_1).
    \end{align}
    In particular, for $\mathsf{P}_i\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ with $\mathscr  W_{p}(\mathsf{P}_0,\mathsf{P}_1)<\infty$ there exists an optimal coupling  $ \mathsf{Q}\in  \mathsf{Cpl}_{s,m}(\mathsf{P}_0,\mathsf{P}_1)$, such that for $\mathsf{P}_t:= \mathsf{gap}^{-1}((T_t)_{\#}\iota( \mathsf{Q}))$ we have \begin{align}
       \mathcal E(\mathsf{P}_t)\leq (1-t)  \mathcal E(\mathsf{P}_0)+t\mathcal E(\mathsf{P}_1)
    \end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.313271+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fa4ba5506ca8a546f0dd53a7615fa627973a89d2a0bfe401cb8cf619c50e60e3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 33 of arXiv:2608.05931, 'Effective Lagrangian regularity and the uniqueness threshold for random H\"older velocity '] There exists a universal constant such that for any martingale difference sequence $D_i$ adapted to a filtration $\mathcal{F}_i$---so $D_i$ is $\mathcal{F}_i$ measurable and $\E[D_i \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}] =0$---we have for all $p \geq 2, n \geq 1$, the bounds
    \begin{equation}

        \Big\|\sum_{i=1}^n D_i\Big\|_{L^p_\omega} \leq C \sup_{ 2  \lor \frac{p}{n} \leq q \leq p} \frac{p}{q} \Big(\frac{n}{p}\Big)^{1/q} \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \big\|\E[|D_i|^q \mid \mathcal{F}_{i-1}]^{1/q}\big\|_{L^\infty_\omega}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.291561+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fa98244e68bce035a08605b149341145b19e34ce1645ae4d483b8a5b3398bb2d","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:rwcre_kernel of arXiv:2603.22652, 'A cohesive account on the ergodic behaviours and scaling limits of Random Walks in Cooling'] Given a resampling map $\tau$ and an i.i.d. environment sequence
$\bar{\omega}\sim\mu^{{\mathbb {N}}}$, the \emph{random walk in cooling random
environment} (RWCRE) is the Markov chain $X=(X_n)_{n\in{\mathbb {N}}_0}$ on ${\mathbb {Z}}$
with transition kernel
\begin{equation}
P^{\bar{\omega},\tau}(X_{n+1}=x+e\mid X_n=x)=
\begin{cases}
\omega_{\ell_n}(x), & e=+1,\\
1-\omega_{\ell_n}(x), & e=-1,
\end{cases}
\quad x\in{\mathbb {Z}},\ n\in{\mathbb {N}}_0,
\end{equation}
where $\ell_n$ is given by (equation eq:ell_def of the paper). We denote by
$P_x^{\bar{\omega},\tau}$ the quenched law and by
\begin{equation}
P_x^{\mu,\tau}(\,\cdot\,):=\int_{[(0,1)^{{\mathbb {Z}}}]^{{\mathbb {N}}}}
P_x^{\bar{\omega},\tau}(\,\cdot\,)\,\mu^{{\mathbb {N}}}(d\bar{\omega})
\end{equation}
the corresponding annealed law.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:37:25.925602+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_fab64729d7a9aaf6d84d7bd77f8084dd56d5762245bb2d4cf9f3904ca99a628a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2608.29585, '2608.29585'] The interpolation-basis filtration on $\cZ_{n,\mathrm{fin}}$ is
separated, and for every $m\ge0$ there is a canonical isomorphism
\begin{equation}
 \cZ_{n,\mathrm{fin}}/\cZ_{n,\mathrm{fin}}^{(m)}
 \cong
 \bigoplus_{\substack{\ell(\lambda)\le n\\|\lambda|\le m}}
 (R_{\mathrm{ev}}/I_m)\sigma_\lambda.
\end{equation}
Consequently, taking inverse limits identifies
\begin{equation}
 \widehat\cZ_n^{\mathrm{BG}}
 \cong
 \prod_{\ell(\lambda)\le n}
 \widehat R_{\mathrm{ev}}^{\mathrm{BG}}\sigma_\lambda
\end{equation}
as topological modules.","status":"unverified"}

